\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen nervy as markets on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  alert; Aussie slips after \uc2 RBA\b0\par\par\uc2 648 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juli 2023\par 07:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2 , July 4 (Reuters) - The yen was firm on Tuesday but remained vulnerable to more weakness, as markets were on alert for signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while the Australian dollar dropped after the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chose to stand pat on interest rates.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  (\uc2 RBA\uc2 ) held interest rates steady at 4.10%, saying it wanted more time to assess the impact of past hikes, but warned further tightening might be needed to bring inflation to heel.\par \par \uc2 Across the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, investors remained on watch for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities to stem yen losses.\par \par \uc2 The yen last fetched 144.64 per dollar in Asian hours, but remained close to last week's eight month low of 145.07 per dollar that prompted Finance Minister \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2  to warn against excessive yen selling.\par \par \uc2 Earlier on Tuesday, Japan's top financial diplomat Masato Kanda said that officials were in close contact with \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  and other overseas authorities almost everyday on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and broader financial markets.\par \par \uc2 "This is sending signals that a coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be coming as yen continues to hover above 144 per dollar," said Charu Chanana, market strategist at Saxo Markets.\par \par \uc2 "A coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually has a longer lasting impact on the yen than a unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have."\par \par \uc2 Japan bought yen in September, its first foray in the market to boost its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 1998, as the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's pledge to retain ultra-loose policy as long as required drove the yen as low as 145 per dollar. It \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in October after the yen plunged to a 32-year low of 151.94.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar slid 0.3% to $0.6654 after the \uc2 RBA\uc2 's decision, reversing its early morning gains.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 RBA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s steady hand is probably only a flesh wound for the Aussie, having been about 60% priced in and with no great rhetorical shifts in the statement," said Sean Callow, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Markets had leant toward a steady outcome given inflation had eased a little more than expected in May, while economists were split on the outcome, with 16 out of 31 polled by Reuters expecting a hike and the rest forecasting the bank to stand pat.\par \par \uc2 Since a surprise pause in April and subsequent hikes in May and June, economists have been mostly divided in recent months over the \uc2 RBA\uc2 's next move.\par \par \uc2 Against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar rose 0.097% at 103.05 after data overnight showed U.S. manufacturing slumped further in June, reaching levels last seen when the nation was reeling from the initial wave of the COVID-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 "We expect the U.S. economy to face a recession starting in Q3 23," \uc2 Kristina Clifton\uc2 , senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2 , adding that it was likely to be a quiet day for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market with no major data releases or \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  speech scheduled. U.S. markets are closed for the July 4 Independence Day holiday.\par \par \uc2 Investor focus this week will be on nonfarm payrolls data along with jobs report for more clues about the broader labour market in the United States. Minutes of the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 's June meeting are also due to be released on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Markets are pricing in a near 87% chance of a 25 basis point hike in the next Federal Reserve meeting at the end of the month, the \uc2 CME\uc2  FedWatch tool showed.\par \par \uc2 The euro was down 0.13% to $1.0897, while sterling was last at $1.2685, down 0.05% on the day. The New Zealand dollar eased 0.15% to $0.614.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Ankur Banerjee in Singapore, additional reporting by Kevin Buckland Editing by Shri Navaratnam) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-7-4T06:19:26.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230704ej7400fth\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
