\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar takes breather on China reopening hopes, U.S. jobs data in focus\b0\par\par\uc2 683 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 2022\par 10:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE/LONDON, Nov 4 (Reuters) - The euro and pound regained some ground on Friday, but were still set for their biggest weekly losses since September, ahead of U.S. jobs data that could underscore the hawkish message Fed chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  delivered this week boosting the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Helping drive the gains was improved investor sentiment following reports China may relax its strict anti-COVID measures, which also caused China's yuan to strengthen sharply.\par \par \uc2 The euro was last up 0.24% at $0.9772, having earlier traded around 0.54% higher. Sterling was up 0.6% at $1.1228, also having pared gains from the Asian session.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, the European common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was still set for a 1.9% drop on the week and sterling a 3.4% decline, both their largest since the third week of September, when Britain's then finance minister \uc2 Kwasi Kwarteng\uc2  sent markets spinning with a now withdrawn set of fiscal policies.\par \par \uc2 The dollar strengthened across the board this week after Federal Reserve chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  on Wednesday said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could continue to increase interest rates if inflation doesn't slow, causing markets to price in a higher peak for U.S. rates.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, markets read a dovish message into authorities' remarks around the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  rate increase on Thursday and \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's last week, while Norway, Canada and Australia's central banks have also recently surprised on the dovish side.\par \par \uc2 "There's a growing perceived chance that the Fed will be the last major \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to throw in the towel and arrest its tightening cycle," said \uc2 Francesco Pesole\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ING.\par \par \uc2 "We think this notion can provide quite sustainable support to the dollar into the new year."\par \par \uc2 U.S. payroll data released later on Friday will provide the latest indication of the health of the U.S. economy. A Reuters survey of economists showed them forecasting an increase of 200,000 jobs last month after a rise of 263,000 in September.\par \par \uc2 "An upside surprise to the data would reinforce the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s higher terminal rate posture and keep the U.S. dollar bid, but softer prints can weigh on the dollar," said Christopher Wong, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 OCBC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In contrast, Friday data showed euro zone business activity contracted last month at the fastest pace since late 2020.\par \par \uc2 CHINA HOPES\par \par \uc2 Friday's 'risk on' move in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as well as commodity and share markets, followed media speculation that China could relax anti-COVID restrictions, which have been hobbling economic activity.\par \par \uc2 China's foreign ministry\uc2  spokesperson said on Friday he was not aware of the media report.\par \par \uc2 The offshore yuan strengthened more than 1% in the Asia session to a one-week peak of 7.2441 per dollar, and last traded at 7.2689.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is the most accessible barometer to digest China's risk sentiment without getting overly complicated," said Stephen Innes, managing partner at SPI Asset Management.\par \par \uc2 "CNH (the yuan traded offshore) will tell you if investors are running hot or cold in China markets. And as is typically the case, this type of 'risk on' move indicated by the yuan will have a magnetic attraction across Asia markets."\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar, also a barometer of sentiment towards China given the countries' close trade ties, was up nearly 1% at $0.6348, while the dollar also slid over 0.5% against the oil sensitive Canadian dollar and Norwegian crown.\par \par \uc2 A higher peak in U.S. rates also spells more pain for the Japanese yen, which has been a victim of widening interest rate differentials as a result of the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's dovishness.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was last down 0.42% at 147.62 yen, with recent moves on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair relatively more subdued on concerns about further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese authorities.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Rae Wee in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  and additional reporting by Winni Zhou in Shanghai; Editing by Simon Cameron-Moore and Mark Potter) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-11-4T10:29:09.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221104eib401p6t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
