\par\par \b \uc2 USD/SGD Falls in Likely Technical Correction -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,356 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2022\par 09:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0659 GMT - USD/SGD falls in the afternoon Asian session in a likely technical correction following the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair's robust gains since the middle of last week. On the daily chart, USD/SGD's relative strength index is in overbought territory although its momentum is bullish, Maybank analysts say in a research report. They peg support at 1.4200 and resistance at 1.4390. The broad USD rally appears to be slowing, but triggers for a more substantial retracement lower might be absent for now, the analysts add. USD/SGD is down 0.2% at 1.4352. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0648 GMT - The FTSE 100 is seen opening higher after a volatile start to the week on Monday amid concerns that U.K. Treasury chief \uc2 Kwasi Kwarteng\uc2  's fiscal policy will send inflation and government debt soaring. IG futures data show the London index opening up 37 points. Kwarteng's mini-budget announcement on Friday sparked turbulence in financial markets, prompting the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  to issue a statement on Monday. The market will "certainly force the BOE to deliver an emergency 100 basis points interest rate hike in the next couple of hours" as the threat of acting at the next meeting won't be enough to stop the pound's meltdown, \uc2 Swissquote Bank\uc2  analyst Ipek Ozkardeskaya writes. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0632 GMT - The global inflation trajectory is becoming even more central for tactical asset allocation, and it could keep volatility elevated until the next set of CPI releases, \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  's analysts say in a note. "A core belief behind our tactical asset allocation approach is that the most consistent factors moving asset prices are business-cycle momentum and monetary-policy decisions," they say. In this elevated-inflation environment, the direction of inflation becomes crucial because it becomes a de facto driver of both those factors, \uc2 JPM\uc2  's analysts say. Elevated inflation directly hurts disposable income and indirectly slows future aggregate demand via the central banks' reaction function, they say. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0623 GMT - Japanese stocks ended higher, led by gains in game, food and consumer goods stocks, following recent market selloffs caused by concerns about policy tightening by major central banks. Konami Group gained 4.0%, \uc2 Suntory Beverage & Food\uc2  climbed 2.2% and Kao added 2.5%. The Nikkei Stock Average rose 0.5% to close at 26571.87. USD/JPY was at 144.40, compared with 144.74 as of Monday 5 p.m. Eastern Time. U.S. economic data would be closely watched. The 10-year Japanese government bond yield rose half a basis point to 0.250%. The 30-year JGB yield gained 6 basis points to 1.435%, its highest level since September 2015. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com; @kosakunarioka)\par \par \uc2 0620 GMT - Slightly more than half respondents in \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  's client survey expect the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to announce quantitative tightening before year-end, \uc2 JPM\uc2  's analysts say in a research note. Specifically, 52% of the respondents expect such an announcement before the end of 2022, while 48% don't expect it, according to the survey. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0455 GMT - Malaysia's 2022 GDP growth is likely to hit 6.4%, says \uc2 World Bank\uc2  in a note. The economic growth will be driven by strong domestic demand due to continued improvements in labor market conditions. Both domestic and foreign travelers are expected to engage in more tourism-related activities, it says, adding that the continuation of multi-year investment projects will probably also support economic expansion. However, the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  says downside risks prevail, especially from the external front, citing a global growth slowdown, unfavorable financial conditions and continued supply chain disruptions. Besides, speculation over the next general election could raise investors' uncertainty in the near term, it says. \uc2 World Bank\uc2  forecasts Malaysia 2023 GDP growth of 4.2%. (yingxian.wong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0235 GMT - Most Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthen against the U.S. dollar in the Asian morning amid risk appetite, driven by gains in U.S. stock futures and due to a likely technical rebound following their recent weakness. However, a further rise in financial-market volatility could lead to more U.S. dollar strength due to its safe-haven status, Carol Kong, senior associate of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy and international economics at \uc2 CBA\uc2  , says in a research note. In particular, \uc2 Atlanta Fed\uc2  president \uc2 Raphael Bostic\uc2  said the rout in U.K. financial markets will put more stress on the U.S. economy, Kong notes. USD/KRW falls 0.2% to 1,427.45, USD/SGD drops 0.2% to 1.4361 and AUD/USD rises 0.5% to 0.6484. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0137 GMT - \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to refrain from selling U.S. Treasurys while undertaking its dollar-selling, yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Totan Research's President and economist Izuru Kato reckons. Instead, the ministry will likely use its $136 billion reserves deposited overseas and with BIS if the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  arises, he says. He estimates that Japan's yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Sep. 22, its first in 24 years, totaled about Y3.6 trillion. The finance ministry is scheduled to release the final figure on Sep. 30. The dollar stood at Y144.40 on Tuesday, compared with Y145.90 before last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .(megumi.fujikawa@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0108 GMT - \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's FTSE Straits Times Index is 0.2% lower at 3174.97, weighed by bank and property stocks. "The aggressive moves by central banks have weighed on equities, with many investors now seeing increased odds of a recession," Phillip Securities analysts say in a note. Movements in the SGD will also be closely watched, after USD/SGD rose 70 pips Monday to 1.4380, a new high this year so far and the highest since April 2020, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analysts say in a note. DBS falls 0.5%, \uc2 OCBC\uc2  declines 0.3% and \uc2 UOB\uc2  is 0.9% lower. Among property developers, \uc2 City Developments\uc2  slips 0.1% and Hongkong Land falls 0.6%. (justina.lee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0039 GMT - Gold prices are higher in Asian trade, rebounding after settling at the lowest price in almost two-and-a-half years overnight. However, a stronger USD could continue to weigh on the precious metal. "The move in the dollar is not over and that should keep the pressure on bullion," Oanda senior market analyst \uc2 Edward Moya\uc2  says in a note. Meanwhile, ANZ analysts reckon that investors are the most bearish on gold in almost four years as central banks increase interest rates to tame inflation. "Its status as a haven asset in times of economic distress has failed to stem the flow of selling," they say in a note. Spot gold rises 0.4% to $1,629.23/oz. (justina.lee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0015 GMT - Japanese stocks are higher in morning trade, led by gains in auto and electronics stocks following recent selloffs caused by concerns about policy tightening by major central banks. \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  is up 1.7% and \uc2 TDK\uc2  is 1.8% higher. Investors are closely watching the yen and Japanese government officials' comments on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . USD/JPY is at 144.33 compared with 143.84 as of Monday's Tokyo stock-market close. The Nikkei Stock Average is up 0.7% at 26620.10. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com; @kosakunarioka)\par \par \uc2 2349 GMT - Japanese stocks may trade rangebound amid continuing uncertainty over policy tightening by major central banks and the economic outlook. Nikkei futures open up 125 points at 26350 on the SGX following Monday's selloffs. Some financial stocks may outperform following gains in \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields overnight. Investors are closely watching the yen and Japanese government officials' comments on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . USD/JPY is at 144.58, compared with 143.84 as of Monday's Tokyo stock market close. The Nikkei Stock Average closed 2.7% lower at 26431.55 on Monday.(kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 27, 2022 03:00 ET (07:00 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220927ei9r000gc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
