\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar nears 1-1/2 mth high vs yen as U.S. yields climb\b0\par\par\uc2 624 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 April 2018\par 06:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Dollar index snaps 4-day losing streak\par \par \uc2 * Treasury yields elevated as equities gain, supporting dollar\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  tightens policy, Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop HK$ fall (Adds details and quotes, updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By Shinichi Saoshiro\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 13 (Reuters) - The dollar neared a 1-1/2-month high against the yen on Friday, as an improvement in investor risk appetite buoyed equities and pushed U.S. yields significantly higher.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was steady at 107.385 yen after gaining more than 0.5 percent overnight. A rise above 107.490 yen would take it to its highest since late February. The greenback has gained about 0.3 percent versus the yen this week.\par \par \uc2 Equities were boosted as Wall Street gained on Thursday in anticipation of strong corporate earnings, and as geopolitical worries eased on U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's suggestion that a military strike on Syria may not be imminent.\par \par \uc2 The dollar had slipped against the yen, a perceived safe-haven, earlier in the week as U.S.-China trade tensions and the possibility of a U.S.-led missile strike on Syria roiled the broader financial markets.\par \par \uc2 "Some of the moves driven by risk aversion that lifted the yen are being unwound. But while risks facing the market may have softened, they have not gone away," said Shin Kadota, senior strategist at \uc2 Barclays\uc2  in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 For now the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was supported by higher Treasury yields, with that of the 10-year surging 5 basis points overnight to its highest since late March.\par \par \uc2 "The dollar had not shown a strong correlation with U.S. yields recently. But the correlation returned somewhat, with \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  taking notice of such a spike in yields," said Junichi Ishikawa, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at IG Securities in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 "Risk aversion in equities will need to keep receding for the dollar to remain supported. There is no change to the equation of 'Trump risk' dictating market direction."\par \par \uc2 The dollar index against a basket of six major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a shade higher at 89.778. It rose 0.2 percent the previous day, ending a four-day losing streak.\par \par \uc2 The euro was little changed at $1.2328 after losing 0.3 percent the previous day, when it ended a four-day winning run.\par \par \uc2 The common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen 0.4 percent this week, supported by comments from \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2  bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials that reinforced expectations towards monetary policy normalisation.\par \par \uc2 The Swiss franc, a perceived safe haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was little changed at 0.9534 per dollar after losing 0.5 percent overnight.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar, sensitive to shifts in risk sentiment, rose 0.3 percent to $0.7777 while the New Zealand dollar was nearly flat at $0.7378.\par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar rose slightly in response to the city-state \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first tightening in six years, before giving up its gains, as the move was well anticipated. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was little changed at 1.3122 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore\uc2  said the economy should remain on a steady expansion path in 2018.\par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar was at 7.8499 per dollar, after \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday and early on Friday by the \uc2 Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) nudged it away from the 7.8500 threshold, the lower end of its trading band.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reached the limit of its trading band due to a steadily widening U.S.-Hong Kong interest rate gap. It was HKMA's first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the lower end of the barrier since the trading band was introduced in 2005. (Reporting by Shinichi Saoshiro; Editing by Richard Pullin and Sam Holmes) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-4-13T05:00:40.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180413ee4d00568\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
