\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Singapore \highlight\uc2  May Need to  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  to Cap  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,333 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Januar 2018\par 08:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0740 GMT - Authorities in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar's strength as the economy is still dependent on exports for growth, says BMI Research. It sees the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  averaging S$1.34/dollar for 2018, versus current trading around S$1.32. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been at 3-year highs of late versus the greenback, which on a broad basis is at its lowest level since 2015. The firm didn't give a prediction at what point the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weakness the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar. (saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; @journosaurabh)\par \par \uc2 0727 GMT - Austria's planned 10-year government bond syndication is expected to take place Thursday, say \uc2 Mizuho\uc2  rates strategists who look forward to a EUR4 billion issuance volume. The \uc2 Mizuho\uc2  strategists see fair value for the new February 2028-dated government bond around 10.5 basis points above the yield of its April 2027-dated peer, or 20 basis points below mid-swaps. The April 2027 bond is trading at a yield of 0.67%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  , while the 10-year mid-swap is trading at 0.965%, according to VWD data. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0726 GMT - China's better-than-expected 2017 GDP growth was largely helped by strong investment momentum, especially in infrastructure and real estate, and partly by improved trade performance, says ANZ economist \uc2 Betty Wang\uc2 . GDP rose 6.9% last year, and she expects growth to edge down to 6.5% in 2018 amid ongoing deleveraging efforts. (lin.zhu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0717 GMT - France's new three-year fixed-rate government bond--the February 2021 OAT--should draw good demand at its launch via auction, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  rates strategist Michael Leister. Demand should be supported by three-year OAT yields rising around 20 basis points up from the lows of early December, Mr. Leister says. France launches the new three-year OAT as part of an overall EUR6.5 billion to EUR7.5 billion auction where it also reopens two existing bonds, the March 2023 OAT and the May 2024 OAT. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0704 GMT - In addition the global developments, \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monetary Policy Committee meeting will be on the top of the domestic agenda today, says Odeabank. "Even though we think a symbolic rate hike from \uc2 CBRT\uc2  would support Turkish financial assets and limit further deterioration in inflation outlook, we expect \uc2 CBRT\uc2  to keep its interest rates unchanged at this meeting," says Odeabank. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision is due at 1100 GMT. "Meanwhile, we think that \uc2 CBRT\uc2  may strengthen its current monetary policy stance by committing to focus on the underlying trend in inflation and core inflation dynamics rather than the fluctuations resulting from base effects," it adds. USD/TRY is down 0.5% at 3.7985. (yeliz.candemir@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0700 GMT - China's economy expanded by 6.9% in 2017, growing at a faster annual pace for the 1st time in 7 years, the National Bureau of Statistics says Thursday. The country's economy grew by 6.8% year on year in the final quarter of 2017, beating a 6.7% increase forecast by economists polled by The Wall Street Journal. (lin.zhu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0653 GMT - An Indian panel of lawmakers headed by Finance Minister Arun Jaitley meets today to review tax rates and some procedural bottlenecks related to GST, says a government official. On the agenda is a rate reduction for a few items such as agricultural equipment and online services, as well as simplification of the law for better compliance, the official adds. The government has been criticized for hurriedly launching the new indirect taxation midyear, necessitating multiple meetings of the GST Council to address the concerns raised by industries and businesses. (rajesh.roy@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0638 GMT - The 1.1% drop in China's Treasurys holdings in November to $1.18 trillion was the result of the government largely refraining from accumulating \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. It was the 2nd drop in 3 months. The tally includes holdings by all Chinese entities, including private enterprises, although it is overwhelming composed of holdings by the \uc2 State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  , whose pile peaked in 2014 as it began to refrain from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Analysts say that meant SAFE wasn't accumulating reserves and had no need to purchase more Treasurys. "I think they will continue to refrain" and "from time to time could adjust the portfolio for rebalancing reasons," says ANZ analyst Khoon Goh. A SAFE spokeswoman stopped short of denying whether it sold holdings but said the US data include others besides SAFE. (chao.deng@wsj.com; @chao_deng)\par \par \uc2 0613 GMT - Amid a pullback in the dollar, notably versus the yen, Japan was among the Asian stock markets which saw afternoon weakness erase morning gains. The Nikkei topped 24000 for the first time since November 1991 this morning but was ultimately taken down by the dollar dropping from Y111.50 to Y111.10. The index ended down 0.4% at 23763.37. Helping the yen as revenue-repatriation season, says Takahiro Sekido, Japan strategist with Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi UFJ. That exporter buying of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair lower. That as Sekido's weekly data show capital outflow by Japanese investors is "muted." Meanwhile, some JGB yields continued to rise amid gains in Treasurys. The 40-year yield is up a basis point at 0.995% while the 10-year remained at 0.08%. (suryatapa.bhattacharya@wsj.com; @SuryatapaB)\par \par \uc2 0401 GMT - US foreclosure-related activity hit its lowest level since 2005 last year, says property database Attom, the latest sign of the housing market's improved health. There were 676,535 properties on which a default notice was filed, an auction scheduled and/or bank repossession occurred in 2017. That was 27% less than a year earlier and 3/4 below 2010's peak. "Thanks to a housing boom driven primarily by a scarcity of supply, which has helped to limit home purchases to the most highly qualified - and low-risk - borrowers, the US housing market has the luxury of playing a version of foreclosure limbo in which it searches for how low foreclosures can go," the firm notes. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 0422 GMT - Asian bond markets continue to feed investors with a variety of dollar-denominated offerings. India's \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  is marketing today 5.5-year and 10-year dollar bonds (BB- by S&P) at 4.875% and 5.875%, respectively. China's \uc2 Geely Automobile\uc2  is offering 1.5 percentage point over similar dated Treasurys for its 5-year (BBB- by S&P) dollar bonds, yielding 3.90%. China South City (expected ratings of B- by S&P/B by \uc2 Fitch\uc2 ) is offering around 7% on its dollar bonds, while Peking University Founder Group is out with 5.05% and 5.65% guidance on its unrated 3-year and 5.5-year dollar bonds. Philippines is out with 10-year sovereign bonds at around 3.30%.(manju.dalal@wsj.com; @manjudalalsg)\par \par \uc2 0255 GMT - A projected acceleration in Chinese CPI this year should result in the \uc2 PBoC\uc2  nudging up rates this year, says Institute of International Finance chief China economist Gene Ma in mirroring the views of some others. He sees CPI being about a half-point higher than 2017 at about 2.2%. With benchmark deposit and lending rates being steady since late 2015, faster CPI "will raise the probability for the \uc2 PBoC\uc2  to hike benchmark rates," adds Ma. Interbank rates and bond yields have been rising, and increased benchmark rates might help curb outflow from deposit accounts and into shadow-banking products, he adds. Beijing has been cracking down on non-bank financial activity. (kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 18, 2018 02:40 ET (07:40 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180118ee1i000jz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
