\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Won hits 9-1/2 month low; rise in U.S. bond yields dents Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 746 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Dezember 2016\par 06:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Won slips 0.4 pct on the day\par     * Taiwan dollar touches 5-1/2 month low\par     * Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sag as US bond yields inch higher\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Masayuki Kitano\par     SINGAPORE, Dec 27 (Reuters) - The South Korean won touched a\par nine-and-a-half-month low against the dollar on Tuesday, with\par most Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  retreating as a rise in U.S. bond yields\par helped bolster the greenback.\par     The won led losses among Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with a\par drop of 0.4 percent on the day. It touched a low of 1,207.4 per\par dollar, the weakest level since March 11.\par     The Taiwan dollar slipped to as low as 32.282 per\par U.S. dollar, its lowest level since July 7. It later pared its\par losses and was last steady on the day.\par     Most other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sagged on a day when the U.S.\par dollar was firmer against major peers, supported by a slight\par rise in the U.S. 10-year Treasury yield in Tuesday's\par Asian trading. \par     Emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have declined broadly since early\par November as U.S. bond yields jumped on expectations that U.S.\par President-elect \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2 's proposals for infrastructure\par spending and tax cuts will boost economic growth and inflation.\par     Worries about Trump's stance on trade have also weighed on\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of export-dependent countries in Asia. \par     Trump has pledged to redraw trade deals to win back American\par jobs, and has threatened to hit Mexico and China with high\par tariffs once he takes office on Jan. 20.\par     Last week, Trump named Peter Navarro, an economist who has\par urged a hard line on trade with China, to head a newly formed\par White House National Trade Council. \par     "It does bring home the point that Trump has got some axe to\par grind... when it comes to bilateral negotiations with China and\par these will not be without impact for the rest of Asia given the\par supply-chain linkages," said Vishnu Varathan, a senior economist\par for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Singapore, referring to Trump tapping Navarro\par for the trade advisory role.\par     Lingering concerns about China's economic outlook are also \par weighing on the South Korean won and the Taiwan dollar, he said.\par     "It is not going to move in a straight line but the tendency\par may be more towards weakness than not for these \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,"\par added Varathan, regarding the outlook for the won and the Taiwan\par dollar in the first quarter of 2017.\par     \par     SINGAPORE DOLLAR\par     The Singapore dollar eased 0.1 percent to 1.4492\par per U.S. dollar. \par     Wariness toward the risk that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the city-state's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  slips to levels beyond\par 1.45 per U.S. dollar helped limit the Singapore dollar's losses.\par  \par     The Singapore dollar had posted mild gains last Friday after\par suspected U.S. dollar-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the city-state's\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  came after the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar touched a seven-year low of 1.4517 on Thursday.\par  \par     There had also been market chatter about suspected\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last Tuesday, when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar slipped to\par as low as 1.4507. \par \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par   Change on the day at   0449 GMT                       \par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                Latest bid   Previous  Pct Move\par                                            day  \par   Japan yen                  117.32     117.11     -0.18\par   Sing dlr                   1.4492     1.4483     -0.06\par   Taiwan dlr                 32.260     32.250     -0.03\par   Korean won                1206.30    1201.40     -0.41\par   Baht                        36.02      35.96     -0.17\par   Peso                       49.800     49.770     -0.06\par   Rupiah                      13433      13445     +0.09\par   Rupee                       67.86      67.74     -0.18\par   Ringgit                    4.4760     4.4725     -0.08\par   Yuan                       6.9495     6.9487     -0.01\par                                                         \par   Change so far in                                      \par  2016                                           \par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                Latest bid   End prev  Pct Move\par                                           year  \par   Japan yen                  117.32     120.30     +2.54\par   Sing dlr                   1.4492     1.4177     -2.17\par   Taiwan dlr            