\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  wobbly;  \highlight22\uc2 Singapore \highlight\uc2  dlr firmer on suspected  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 669 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Dezember 2016\par 08:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar firm after suspected c.bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par     * Move comes after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar set 7-year low Thursday\par     * Won hits 9-month low; Taiwan dollar touches 5-month low\par     * Other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  languish near recent troughs\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Masayuki Kitano\par     SINGAPORE, Dec 23 (Reuters) - The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar edged\par higher on Friday after suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par though Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  looked wobbly and remained vulnerable\par amid expectations the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  will pick up the pace\par of interest rate rises in 2017.\par     The Singapore dollar rose 0.3 percent to 1.4465 per\par U.S. dollar, pulling away from Thursday's low of 1.4517, which\par was its weakest level since August 2009. \par     The Singapore dollar held firm after pushing higher earlier\par on Friday.\par     "There is talk of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  activity in the markets, and\par with year end flow and liquidity lower, we are possibly seeing\par some exaggerated moves," said Saktiandi Supaat, head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par research for Maybank in Singapore.\par     "That could partly explain some of the sharp intraday SGD\par strength against the U.S. dollar," he added.\par     The South Korean won and Taiwan dollar both added to their\par recent losses. The won hit a fresh 9-month low at 1,204.5\par  per dollar, while the Taiwan dollar touched its\par lowest level in nearly five months at 32.172.\par     Foreign investor outflows from Taiwanese equities are seen\par as one of the catalysts behind the Taiwan dollar's recent\par weakness. \par     Foreign investors may have pulled some money out of South\par Korean bonds recently and that may be weighing on the won, said\par Hirofumi Suzuki, an economist for Sumitomo Mitsui Banking\par Corporation in Singapore.\par     "Domestic investors (in South Korea) are probably buying\par bonds on the back of a worsening economic outlook... On the\par other hand, foreign investors are pulling funds out of emerging\par markets," Suzuki said.\par     Emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have declined broadly since early\par November, as the dollar and U.S. bond yields jumped on\par expectations incoming U.S. President Donald Trump's plans to\par boost infrastructure spending and cut taxes will boost economic\par growth and inflation.\par     Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have come under renewed pressure after the\par Fed raised interest rates last week for the first time in a\par year, and also signalled three hikes in 2017. \par     Higher U.S. interest rates and bond yields can reduce the\par attractiveness of investing in emerging market assets and spur\par capital outflows from these countries.\par     \par     SINGAPORE DOLLAR\par     The Singapore dollar showed little reaction after November \par industrial production came in stronger than expected.\par    Singapore's industrial production in November rose at the\par fastest annual pace in more than 2-1/2 years as electronics\par output jumped, a welcome boost to an economy flirting with\par recession. \par \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR    \par   Change on the day at   0700 GMT                     \par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                Latest bid   Previous     Pct\par                                            day    Move\par   Japan yen                  117.37     117.53   +0.14\par   Sing dlr                   1.4465     1.4510   +0.31\par   Taiwan dlr                 32.162     32.030   -0.41\par   Korean won                1202.87    1199.10   -0.31\par   Baht                        36.01      36.02   +0.04\par   Peso                       49.910     49.990   +0.16\par   Rupiah                      13457      13450   -0.05\par   Rupee                       67.87      68.01   +0.21\par   Ringgit                    4.4770     4.4760   -0.02\par   Yuan                       6.9499     6.9440   -0.08\par                                                       \par   Change so far                                       \par  in 2016                                        \par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                Latest bid   End prev     Pct\par                                           year    Move\par   Japan yen                  117.37     120.30   +2.50\par   Sing dlr                   1.4465     1.4177   -1.99\par   Taiwan dlr                 32.162     33.066   +2.81\par   Korean won                1202.87    1172.50   -2.52\par   Baht                        36.01      36.00   -0.02\par   Peso                        49.91      47.06   -5.71\par   Rupiah                      13457      13785   +2.44\par   Rupee                       67.87      66.15   -2.53\par   Ringgit                    4.4770     4.2935   -4.10\par   Yuan                       6.9