\par\par \b \uc2 REFILE-EXCLUSIVE-Malaysia demands foreign banks commit to stop offshore ringgit trading - sources\b0\par\par\uc2 641 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 November 2016\par 03:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Corrects day lettere was sent to Sunday from Saturday.)\par \par \uc2 By Saikat Chatterjee and Jongwoo Cheon\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG/SINGAPORE, Nov 16 (Reuters) - Foreign banks have been asked to make a written commitment to \uc2 Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop trading the ringgit in the offshore non-deliverable forwards market in the bank's latest move to protect a weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , banking sources said.\par \par \uc2 Foreign banks have been sent a form letter to sign, which asks them for an "unconditional representation and commitment" to stop trading in any offshore Malaysian ringgit non-deliverable forwards or offshore derivatives.\par \par \uc2 The letters were sent by banks in Malaysia who hold bonds and other Malaysian assets as custodians for foreign banks. The form letters are addressed to \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Two separate sources at banks confirmed getting the form letter, which Reuters reviewed.\par \par \uc2 The letters also asks financial institutions to provide a detailed plan to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  if they need to make ringgit transactions onshore and to seek help from Malaysian financial institutions for any \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  transaction needs.\par \par \uc2 Bank Negara\uc2  in a statement to Reuters on Wednesday confirmed it has made the request through banks in Malaysia.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2  has requested through onshore banks that any non-resident banks, which transact in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, to attest that they are not and will not engage in NDF related transactions," the \uc2 Bank Negara\uc2  statement said.\par \par \uc2 Foreign holdings account for 40 percent of the total outstanding bond market in Malaysia, one of the largest foreign ownerships in Asia.\par \par \uc2 Foreigners have been fleeing the Malaysian market in a global bond rout following \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's election as U.S. President last week, which sent the dollar soaring and has hit emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  particularly hard.\par \par \uc2 Trump is expected to adopt policies that are likely to increase interest rates faster than previously thought.\par \par \uc2 Investors typically use the liquid NDF markets in Singapore and Hong Kong to exchange ringgit for dollars because of the many restrictions in the domestic market.\par \par \uc2 NON-INTERNATIONAL \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 The letters follow a \uc2 Bank Negara\uc2  statement on Sunday saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would "re-enforce" existing rules against offshore trading of the ringgit. The bank said the ringgit is a non-internationalised \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and thus trading it in the overseas NDF markets "is not recognised".\par \par \uc2 Malaysia's ringgit touched a 10-month low to $4.34 to the dollar at around 1100 GMT. But in the offshore NDF markets late last week, the ringgit dropped to a 12-year low.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move is not surprising, given how much the ringgit has lost recently, said Nordea Markets' chief analyst Amy Yuan Zhuang in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "It sounds like a desperate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Bank Negara\uc2 's comparatively small \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves has left \uc2 Bank Negara\uc2  with fewer options, she said. "It needs such measures more than other Asian central banks."\par \par \uc2 Malaysia's foreign reserves stood at $97.8 billion at the end of October.\par \par \uc2 The head of an emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading desk at a Western bank in Hong Kong said it would be very difficult for \uc2 Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to crack down on NDF transactions because they have no oversight.\par \par \uc2 "Maybe, they'll have some success with onshore banks and their overseas trades but it would be difficult for them to check such trades at the big foreign banks offshore," he said.\par \par \uc2 Stephen Innes, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader for broker OANDA in Singapore said the one-month spread for NDFs was still very wide. "It was almost untradeable."\par \par \uc2 "My feeling is that they are trying to effectively put on a capital control, as direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be difficult because of the fragile reserves situation," he said. (Additional reporting by Praveen Menon. Editing by Bill Tarrant.) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-11-17T03:25:40.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020161116ecbg00i8f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
