\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dollar Falls as Much as 1.2% on Brexit Lead -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,832 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2016\par 03:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0155 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Singapore dollar slumped as much as 1.2% against the U.S. dollar Friday, as the market reacted to preliminary results showing voters' bias for Britain to leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2 . The pound collapsed, sending the U.S. dollar rocketing across the board, as punters were positioned toward an outcome favoring the status quo for the U.K.. Current vote count results indicate 50.0% want Britain to stay in the \uc2 EU\uc2 . The USD/SGD has eased as the polls now point to a better chance for Britain to stay, but is unlikely to fall below Thursday's low of 1.3313. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday to dampen the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar when it rose to a one-year high of 1.3313. Traders said price action strongly suggested the MAS doesn't want the Singapore dollar to appreciate. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had only risen 0.5% on a daily basis when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was said to have stepped in.\par \par \uc2  The USD/SGD is now at 1.3455 from its Thursday closing of 1.3368, representing a 0.7% weakening of the Singapore dollar. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0149 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The U.K.'s possible exit from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  will have a big impact on Japanese companies that have European headquarters in the U.K., says Takashi Hiratsuka, a trading group leader at the asset-management division of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Resona Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "It'd be a big burden if they had to do business in the [European] bloc from a foreign country. There would be significant implications to many Japanese companies that are based in the U.K. and doing their business around Europe. They might have to change their strategy there." The Nikkei is down 0.1% at 16230.16 amid uncertainty over the U.K.'s fate in the European bloc. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0145 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] If the U.K. were to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the benchmark 10-year Japan government bond yield would likely fall to around minus 0.2% (it's currently at 0.155%) as a Brexit would raise expectations for additional easing from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2 , says Deutsche strategist Makoto Yamashita. "In case of leave, there is the possibility that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of England\f28 \uc2 \f2  and other central banks will hold an emergency meeting to prepare for any crisis situation," he says. If the yen jumps along with sharp falls in the pound, speculation about joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will likely grow, preventing a one-sided jump in the yen, he adds. (megumi.fujikawa@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0139 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The EUR/JPY is turning down and has broken below both 20-period and 50-period moving averages. And the relative strength index is badly directed. As long as 120.00 holds as the key resistance, a drop toward 117.85 and even 117.20 is possible. However, if the pair stands above 120.00, further challenge of 122.00 is likely. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9] (analysts@tradingcentral.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0137 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The EUR/GBP is posting a strong rebound and is expected to continue its bounce. The 20-period moving average has just crossed above the 50-period one and the relative strength index is well directed. Further upside is therefore expected with 0.7850 and 0.7920 as the next targets. Only a break below the horizontal support at 0.7700 would allow for a retest of 0.7635 and 0.7595 in extension. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9] (analysts@tradingcentral.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0136 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] NZD/USD Intraday: Turning down. The pair has struck against its key resistance at 0.7255, and is now turning down. Meanwhile, the relative strength index is below its neutrality area at 50, and is displaying strong downside momentum. To conclude, a break below 0.7170 would trigger a drop towards 0.7150 at first, and then towards 0.7115. Alternative scenario, above 0.7255, look for further upside with 0.7280 & 0.7300 as targets. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9] (analysts@tradingcentral.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0135 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Counting so far in the U.K. referendum has served as a wake up call for markets, with some nervous hours ahead, says Nick Bishop, head of Australian fixed income at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Aberdeen Asset Management\f28 \uc2 \f2 . His firm went into the referendum positioned neutrally, and that strategy looks to have been a wise one given the volatility in credit default swaps and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets since the close of polls. The plunge in the pound was reminsicent of market volatility in the 2011 European debt crisis, he adds. The referendum has "still clearly been shown to be a coin toss," he says. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0134 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/CHF Intraday: Upside prevails. The pair broke above its 20-period and 50-period moving averages and is holding on the upside. Additionally, the 20-period moving average crossed above the 50-period one, which is a positive signal. Besides, the relative strength lacks downward momentum. To sum up, as long as 0.9565 is not broken, further upside is expected with the next horizontal resistance at 0.9655 & 0.9670 in extension. Alternatively, only a break below the horizontal support at 0.9565 would open the way to further downsides with 0.9540 & 0.9515 as targets. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9] (analysts@tradingcentral.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0130 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Hang Seng is expected to open lower despite latest results showing that the remain camp has a lead in the British referendum on \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  membership. However, roughly only 4% of the total votes have being declared. The Hang Seng futures are slowly recovering to trade down 0.8% from 1.5% earlier after the leave camp showed a lead. Shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC Holdings PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  (0005.HK) and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Cheung Kong Infrastructure Holdings Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (1038.HK) are expected to open lower as there was some relief buying in the stocks ahead of the vote post selling in the lead up. Financial firms listed in Hong Kong would be hurt as well due to the sharp turmoil in global equity markets. Importantly, sharp moves in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , could trigger margin calls in trade positions from the mainland that have been made on borrowed capital. Property stocks in Hong Kong could have a mixed blessing. Mainland investors seeking to diversify their renminbi holding could find Britain an attractive proposition if the pound weakens following a Brexit, but at the cost of Hong Kong and Australia. But Chinese property developers listed in Hong Kong could increase their investments and projects in the U.K. (kenan.machado@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0129 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Friday's yuan benchmark rate was set at 6.5776 versus Thursday's 6.5658, representing a surprisingly weaker domestic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The yuan was marked down by 0.2% against the dollar, despite the U.S. dollar index closing lower on Thursday. The USD/CNY daily parity rate typically matches the overnight move of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 ICE\f28 \uc2 \f2  U.S. dollar index, which closed at a nearly two-month low overnight. The index is however up sharply today, due to the sharp downturn of the pound and the euro, on the incoming results of the U.K. referendum. Latest results show that nearly 53% of voters want the U.K. to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Counting of votes is still on and the final results will be known later today. The weaker yuan fixing may suggest that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  engineered it to pre-empt yuan strength at the conclusion of the U.K. referendum. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  may have tried to position the yuan's fixing based on earlier indications that Britain would choose the status quo, which would have battered safe haven assets and lifted Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and other risky assets. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0129 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Gold prices rallied by more than 2% in Asia trade amid growing uncertainty over the results of the ongoing vote count on Britain's membership with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Spot gold rose 2.3% to $1,285.01/ounce, taking it closer to a 22-month high touched last week. (biman.mukherji@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 23 Jun 2016 21:55 ET \par Singapore Dollar Falls as Much as 1.2% on Brexit -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0127 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Bourse operator \f28 \uc2 \f2 Singapore Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2  (S68.SG) is battening down hatches in preparation for volatility in the markets from a referendum in the U.K. The exchange says it has raised margins on derivatives trades in order to counter volatility surrounding the vote, but notes that this is a standard measure during big global risk events. Traders will now have to put up more cash to cover positions, which could deter some from betting. Early vote counts on the U.K. referendum show more votes in favor of the U.K. leaving the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  rather than staying in it. (jake.watts@wsj.com; Twitter: @jmwatts_) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 June 23, 2016 21:55 ET (01:55 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160624ec6o0007j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
