\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Ringgit leads Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  losses as oil slides; strong GDP supports Singapore dlr\b0\par\par\uc2 703 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2016\par 06:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Ringgit hits 3-week low, Malaysia bond prices slide\par     * Won near 5-1/2-year trough; \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  caution limits\par     * \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dlr up after Q4 growth beats expectations\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, Feb 24 (Reuters) - Malaysia's ringgit hit a\par three-week low on Wednesday, leading losses among emerging Asian\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as oil prices extended their slide, dashing\par investors' appetite for riskier assets.\par     The Chinese yuan eased as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set\par its daily guidance rate weaker again. The South Korean\par won hovered a near 5-1/2-year trough on selling from\par offshore funds.\par     Singapore's dollar, however, found support from\par stronger-than-expected fourth-quarter economic growth data.\par  \par     Oil prices extended losses in Asia after top exporter Saudi\par Arabia ruled out production cuts and industry data showed a\par further build in U.S. crude stockpiles. That dented risky\par assets including Asian stocks.\par     "Crude prices rule direction of overall risk assets," said\par Jeong My-young, Samsung Futures' research head in Seoul.\par     "Low oil prices weaken inflation expectations and have big\par impact on monetary policy amid sluggish global demand."\par     \par     RINGGIT\par     The ringgit lost 1.2 percent to 4.2445 per dollar, its\par weakest since Feb. 3, as falling crude prices underscored\par concerns about Malaysia's shrinking gas and oil revenues.\par     Most government bond prices fell with the 5-year\par yield up to 3.495 percent, its highest since Jan. 12.\par     The Malaysian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  came under further pressure from\par dollar demand linked to daily fixing.\par     The unit is seen weakening to 4.2610, the 50 percent\par Fibonacci retracement of its appreciation from January to\par February, analysts said.\par     Against the neighbouring Singapore dollar, the\par ringgit slid 1.1 percent to 3.0156, its weakest since Jan. 22.\par     \par     WON\par     The won fell as much as 0.5 percent to 1,236.7 per dollar,\par compared to its low of 1,239.6 on Feb. 19, which was the weakest\par since June 2010.\par     The South Korean \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  recovered some of earlier losses\par on growing caution over possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem further\par losses in the worst-performing Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  so far this year.\par     Last Friday, the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities were\par suspected to have sold about $2 billion, traders said, as the\par won hit the 5-1/2-year low. \par     \par     SINGAPORE DOLLAR\par     The Singapore dollar started the day weaker but turned\par firmer after data showed the economy grew an annualised 6.2\par percent in the October-December quarter.\par     That was stronger than the government's initial estimate of\par 5.7 percent and a forecast of a 4.0 percent in a Reuters poll.\par     That caused investors to cut bearish bets, which they had\par increased after Singapore on Tuesday after the city-state\par lowered its headline inflation forecast for this year after a\par collapse in global oil prices. \par     Still, economist saw risks of further stimulus, limiting\par gains in the Singapore dollar.\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0510 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       111.75         112.12       +0.33\par   Sing dlr        1.4071         1.4075       +0.03\par   Taiwan dlr      33.258         33.485       +0.68\par   Korean won     1233.80        1231.10       -0.22\par   Baht             35.72          35.74       +0.04\par   Peso             47.70          47.64       -0.13\par   Rupiah        13440.00       13420.00       -0.15\par   Rupee            68.57          68.57       +0.01\par   Ringgit         4.2300         4.1955       -0.82\par   Yuan            6.5323         6.5264       -0.09\par  \par   Change so far in 2016\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       111.75         120.30       +7.65\par   Sing dlr        1.4071         1.4177       +0.75\par   Taiwan dlr      33.258         33.066       -0.58\par   Korean won     1233.80        1172.50       -4.97\par   Baht             35.72          36.00       +0.78\par   Peso             47.70          47.06       -1.33\par   Rupiah        13440.00       13785.00       +2.57\par   Rupee            68.57          66.15       -3.52\par   Ringgit         4.2300         4.2935       +1.50\par   Yuan            6.5323         6.4936       -0.59\par  \par  (Additional reporting by IFR Markets' Catherine Tan; Editing by\par Kim Coghill)\f28 \par \par \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-2-24T06:35:56.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160224ec2o00env\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
