\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Weak dollar lifts Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 ; yuan, won jump\b0\par\par\uc2 731 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2015\par 08:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Yuan highest since Aug devaluation on strong midpoint\par     * Won at 3-month high; suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  limits gains\par     * Ringgit, rupiah down on profit-taking\par     * \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dlr dips, c.bank seen easing policy on Wed\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, Oct 12 (Reuters) - Most emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par gained on Monday as the dollar broadly fell due to growing\par doubts that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  will raise interest rates\par this year.\par     The Chinese yuan hit its strongest level since\par its surprise devaluation on Aug. 11, after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set\par a firmer midpoint. The South Korean won \par touched a three-month peak on catch-up demand after a long\par weekend.\par     Some Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , especially the recent outperformers\par such as the Malaysian ringgit, eased, but they pared\par losses against the U.S. dollar which is near a three-week\par low versus a basket of major units.\par     Asian stocks extended gains as investors unwound bearish\par bets on commodities and emerging markets trades. That came as\par expectations have been waning since disappointing September U.S.\par jobs data.\par     "The rally in risk-proxies may continue in the short-term,\par so USD/Asia ex-Japan could see some downward pressure," said\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Christopher Wong\f28 \uc2 \f2 , senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst for Maybank in Singapore.\par     Wong, however, doubted whether a long-term appreciation in\par emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was possible, given a slowdown in \par regional economies. It was also too early to rule out\par possibility of a U.S. interest rate increase this year, he\par added.\par     \par     WON\par     The won ended local trade up 1.4 percent to 1,143.5 per\par dollar, its strongest since July 15.\par     With local financial markets were closed on Friday for a\par holiday, offshore funds chased the won to catch up with other\par Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  which rose.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities were suspected of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par to slow down the won's appreciation, traders said.\par     \par     RINGGIT\par     The ringgit fell in thin trade as traders saw its recent\par gains as excessive.\par     The Malaysian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  jumped nearly 7 percent against the\par greenback, the largest weekly appreciation since September 1998,\par when Malaysia country pegged the ringgit at 3.8000 to the dollar\par until 2005.\par     Industrial production rose 3.0 percent from a year earlier\par in August, on higher manufacturing and mining output, but was\par below market expectations. \par     \par     SINGAPORE DOLLAR\par     The Singapore dollar slid ahead of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's policy announcement on Wednesday.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore\f28 \uc2 \f2  was expected to ease its\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate-based policy to support an economy that\par may have slipped into a recession in the third quarter for the\par first time since the global financial crisis. \par     Some hedge funds, however, cut back bearish bets on the\par Singapore dollar at 1.4030 versus the U.S. dollar, tracking the\par greenback's overall weakness.\par     \par     RUPIAH\par     Indonesia's rupiah eased as traders booked profits\par with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's 200-day moving average at 13,306 per dollar.\par     The rupiah has been closing daily sessions weaker than the\par average since September 2014.\par     Last week, the rupiah jumped 9.2 percent against the dollar,\par its largest weekly gain since July 2001, Thomson Reuters data\par showed.\par         \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0600 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       120.16         120.25       +0.07\par   Sing dlr        1.3990         1.3949       -0.29\par   Taiwan dlr      32.376         32.850       +1.46\par   Korean won     1142.83        1159.00       +1.41\par   Baht             35.52          35.54       +0.06\par   Peso             45.81          45.87       +0.13\par   Rupiah        13430.00       13410.00       -0.15\par   Rupee            64.75          64.74       -0.02\par   Ringgit         4.1640         4.1355       -0.68\par   Yuan            6.3249         6.3453       +0.32\par  \par   Change so far in 2015\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       120.16         119.66       -0.42\par   Sing dlr        1.3990         1.3260       -5.22\par   Taiwan dlr      32.376         31.718       -2.03\par   Korean won     1142.83        1099.30       -3.81\par   Bah