\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dollar at Record High Versus Tumbling Ringgit -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 189 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 2015\par 05:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0322 GMT [Dow Jones] One Singapore dollar now buys 2.9200 Malaysia ringgit, setting a record valuation for the SGD/MYR pair as the ringgit tumbles to yet another 17-year low absent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Both the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar and the Malaysia ringgit are weaker versus the US dollar Friday, due to returning fears of a US interest rate rise in September. But the ringgit is plagued by deeper domestic problems centered on the weak standing of Malaysia's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. The low level of reserves makes it untenable for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep selling US dollars on the spot market to deter ringgit weakness. Punters have latched on to this rhetoric, attacking the ringgit at its weakest in 17-years and plunging it to new lows, nearly on a daily basis since the start of August. SGD/MYR is now at 2.9200 from its Thursday close of 2.8685, representing a 1.8% move. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 13, 2015 23:22 ET (03:22 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150814eb8e000eq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
