\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Firm dollar hurts Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  but Taiwan dlr at over 4-month high\b0\par\par\uc2 723 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2015\par 06:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Won snaps 4-session winning streak on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  caution\par     * Philippine peso down on slow inflation data\par     * \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dlr dips; c.bank to announce policy on Apr 14\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, April 7 (Reuters) - Most emerging Asian\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  slid on Tuesday as the dollar recovered much of its\par post-payrolls losses.\par     South Korea's won snapped a four-session rising\par streak on caution over possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities.\par     The Philippine peso eased on slower inflation in\par March. \par     The Singapore dollar dipped amid uncertainties over\par whether the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would ease policy on April 14.\par  \par     Taiwan's dollar, however, jumped to its strongest\par in more than four months on catch-up plays after holidays.\par     The U.S. dollar rose against a basket of six major\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on weakness in the euro and the yen.\par     The U.S. services sector showed encouraging strength in\par exports and employment in March, according to an industry\par report. That added to hope that the economy can quickly recover\par from a first-quarter slowdown. \par     "What is supporting USD/Asia is not just whether U.S.\par economy outperforms or U.S. rates lift sooner or later," said\par Andy Ji, Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commonwealth Bank of\par Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Singapore.\par     "It is still risk aversion with all the uncertainties," Ji\par added, referring to bearish factors such as the slowing Chinese\par economy.\par         \par     WON\par     The won fell as the dollar's rebound caused traders to cut\par bullish bets in the South Korean \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Caution grew over possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as the won\par  on Monday hit 9.0658 against the yen, its strongest\par since March 2008.\par     South Korea's exporters compete against Japanese rivals in\par overseas markets for key products such as cars.\par     Investors are also awaiting \f28 \uc2 \f2 South Korea's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par policy meeting on Thursday.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Korea\f28 \uc2 \f2  is widely expected to keep interest rates\par unchanged this week but lower them once more in coming months if\par the economy continues to struggle for traction, a Reuters survey\par showed. \par     \par     PHILIPPINE PESO\par     The peso eased as data showed March inflation slowed to 2.4\par percent, lower than February's 2.5 percent and a market forecast\par of 2.6 percent.\par     Still, the core inflation rose to 2.7 percent, the fastest\par since October, according to the data.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's monetary policy remains appropriate but\par it is ready to make adjustments if needed, its chief said.\par  \par     \par     TAIWAN DOLLAR\par     The Taiwan dollar rose as much as 1.0 percent to 30.986 per\par the U.S. dollar, its strongest since Nov. 28.\par     Traders chased the island's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to reflect strength in\par regional units after disappointing U.S. March jobs data on\par Friday knocked the greenback. Taiwan's financial markets were\par closed from Friday to Monday.\par     Taiwan stocks also gained with foreign investors\par buying them. Exporters also purchased the Taiwan dollar for\par settlements.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was spotted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem gains in\par the best-performing Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  so far this year, traders\par said.\par \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0440 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       119.59         119.53       -0.05\par   Sing dlr        1.3558         1.3545       -0.10\par   Taiwan dlr      30.989         31.303       +1.01\par   Korean won     1088.90        1084.80       -0.38\par   Baht             32.50          32.50       +0.00\par   Peso             44.53          44.39       -0.30\par   Rupiah        12978.00       12957.00       -0.16\par   Rupee            62.32          62.18       -0.22\par   Ringgit         3.6310         3.6315       +0.01\par   Yuan            6.1923         6.1950       +0.04\par  \par   Change so far in 2015\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       119.59         119.66       +0.05\par   Sing dlr        1.3558         1.3260       -2.20\par   Taiwan dlr      30.989         31.718       +2.35\par   Korean won     1088.90        1099.30       +0.96\par   Baht             32.50          32.90       +1.23\par   Peso             44.53          4