\par\par \b \uc2 POLL-Most analysts now see Singapore c.bank holding off on further easing in April\b0\par\par\uc2 775 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 11:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * 12 of 17 analysts expect no easing of MAS policy in April\par     * Five expect MAS to ease via re-centering of SGD NEER band\par     * Seven see chance of wider band to accommodate volatility\par \par     By Jongwoo Cheon and Masayuki Kitano\par     SINGAPORE, March 25 (Reuters) - Singapore's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is\par expected to maintain its stance of allowing the local dollar's\par appreciation in April, a Reuters poll showed on Wednesday, a\par marked change from expectations just a few weeks ago that it\par would ease policy again to ward off deflationary pressures.\par     Twelve out of 17 economists and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analysts surveyed\par expect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore\f28 \uc2 \f2  (MAS) to hold off\par from easing its exchange-rate based monetary policy at its\par semi-annual policy review expected around mid-April.\par     The remaining five expect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to ease policy by\par lowering the mid-point of the Singapore dollar's policy band.   \par  \par     Rather than setting interest rates, the MAS manages monetary\par policy by letting the Singapore dollar rise or fall against the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of its main trading partners within a secret trading\par band based on its nominal effective exchange rate (NEER).\par     A similar poll early this month showed most market watchers\par expected the MAS to ease policy again in April after a surprise\par move on Jan. 28 at an unscheduled meeting. But analysts in the\par latest survey noted core inflation has since inched up from a\par five-year low.\par     Core inflation, which excludes car-related and accommodation\par costs, rose 1.3 percent in February from a year earlier, up from\par January's 1.0 percent, the lowest since March 2010.\par     The MAS focuses more on core inflation than broader,\par headline consumer prices, which have fallen for four straight\par months largely due to sliding global oil prices as well as lower\par housing and car-related prices.\par     Among the 12 analysts expecting no policy easing, seven said\par they either expected or saw some chance of the MAS widening the\par policy band to accommodate \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market fluctuations. \par     "We believe the MAS is unlikely to further ease policy in\par April, as the revised inflation forecast still holds," analysts\par at \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a research note.\par     "Instead, we expect it to widen the band to +/-3 percent to\par increase policy flexibility, given rising economic uncertainty\par and financial market volatility."\par     Currently, analysts estimate the band is 2 percent of the\par mid-point on either side.\par     Some analysts also believe the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is likely to\par maintain the current policy to stabilise the Singapore dollar as\par global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets grow more volatile.\par     After January's easing, the Singapore dollar NEER hit the\par bottom of the policy band, analysts said. The city-state's unit\par  has lost some 3 percent against the U.S. dollar, making\par it the third-worst performing Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  so far this year,\par Thomson Reuters data showed.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was spotted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar, resulting in tighter domestic liquidity\par conditions, some analysts said. On Tuesday, the benchmark\par three-month interest rate rose above 1 percent for the first\par time in more than six years. \par     Further easing may exacerbate weakness in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par dollar and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may have to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  further,\par analysts said.\par     \par     EASING CHANCES\par     Still, some economists expect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to ease\par policy by lowering the mid-point of the policy band, due to a\par subdued outlook for both inflation and economic growth.\par     "We are looking for two things. Number one, a move lower in\par the mid-point of the band...and two, also a change in slope to\par zero percent. So a double move," said Vaninder Singh, an\par economist for RBS in Singapore.\par     Exports fell much more than expected last month as China's\par slowing economy hit the trade-dependent state.\par     More than 20 central banks around the world have taken\par advantage of cooling inflation to ease policy so far this year\par to boost sluggish growth and fend off deflation risks.   \par     \par     INDIVIDUAL EXPECTATIONS ON POLICY IN APRIL\par                                  Policy move    Risk\par  ANZ                             Re-centering\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2    Re-centering\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\f28 \uc2 \f2  UFJ    Band-widening\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2                         No change\par  BNP Paribas                     Band-widening\par  Capital Economics               No change\par  CIMB                            No change\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2   No change      Band-widening\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2                             Band-widening\par  ING                             No change\par  Maybank                         No change      Band-widening\par  Mizuho Bank                     No change      Band-widening \par  Oversea-Chinese Banking Corp    No change\par  RBS                             Re-centering,\par                                  reduce slope to zero pct \par  Standard Chartered              Re-centering\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 United Overseas Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2             Re-centering \par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2                          Band-widening  Re-centering or\par                                                 flat slope\par  \par \par  (Editing by Kim Coghill)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-3-28T10:01:52.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150325eb3p00cyp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
