\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dollar Hits New Low as EUR Falls, MAS Meeting Looms -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,830 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 02:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0132 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/SGD rallied again overnight to a fresh 4.5-year high of 1.3908 as the euro tumbled to a 12-year low, sending the U.S. dollar index skywards. But USD/SGD has since retreated to its current 1.3850 on market chatter that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market to buffer the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against excessive intraday weakness. USD/SGD has been on the rise due to hawkish forecasts for the timing of the first increase in the U.S. interest rate. USD/SGD may test the major round-figure trading barrier of 1.4000 as the Singapore monetary policy meeting in April approaches - punters are betting that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could opt to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to battle deflation and slowing growth. USD/SGD is now 1.3850 from its Tuesday close of 1.3901. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0122 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/TWD has tentatively turned bullish on the daily chart as the pair ended Tuesday just inside the daily Bollinger uptrend channel. A Wednesday close above 31.680 would cement the positive chart cue and possibly boost the pair toward 32.000 in the near term. The speculative offshore market has been turning bullish over the past few days and this positive-USD tone has filtered into the spot market. Since Monday, the price gap between the offshore nondeliverable one-month forward contract and the spot contract price has widened to 0.050 from 0.020, implying stronger U.S. dollar demand. The benchmark U.S. dollar index surged to a 11.5-year high overnight as the euro sank, setting a positive tone for most USD/Asia pairs Wednesday. USD/TWD officially closed Tuesday at 31.566. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0118 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  is likely to set the daily USD/CNY benchmark rate higher on account of the overnight surge of the U.S. dollar index as the euro tumbled to new 12-year lows. If the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  fixes the daily benchmark much higher than Tuesday's 6.1572 there could be another attempt by spot USD/CNY to enter the daily Bollinger uptrend channel at 6.2673. Activation of the bullish chart signal could send USD/CNY toward 6.2800 in the near term. China releases its February industrial output data and retail sales figures at 0530 GMT. Recent data has been disappointing, though the consumer price index released Tuesday showed a bigger than expected rise. USD/CNY closed Tuesday at 6.2620. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0107 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\f28 \uc2 \f2  is likely to keep its official cash rate on hold at 3.5% on Thursday as "after hiking rates by 100 basis points in 2014, low inflation from falling oil prices gives the RBNZ room to sit pat for most of 2015," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Analytics. Dry conditions have weakened agriculture production, while the "precipitous decline" in dairy prices is hurting export receipts. Imported inflation, which accounts for about 50% of the CPI, has also fallen on the back of the relatively strong kiwi and lower oil prices. "With concern about international competitiveness increasing, the RBNZ could be tempted to join the global monetary easing wave, which includes 21 central banks in 2015," it says. However, "such a scenario is unlikely." Domestic demand remains on firm footing thanks to strength in residential construction and retail trade. "A new easing cycle could fan asset bubbles, particularly in the frothy housing market." (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; Twitter: @FarroHoward) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0104 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] EUR/GBP likely to consolidate with bearish bias near-term after hitting seven-year low 0.7081 this morning. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD bearish; stochastics stays suppressed at oversold levels; five- and 15-day moving averages declining. Support at the psychological 0.7000 line; breach would expose downside to 0.6891 (Oct. 9, 2007 low), then to 0.6677 (July 26, 2007 low). Resistance at 0.7148 (hourly chart), then at 0.7180 (Tuesday's high); breach would temper negative near-term view, targeting 0.7219 (Monday's high). Spot EUR/GBP is at 0.7096 versus 0.7099 late Tuesday. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0103 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/CAD likely to trade in higher range Wednesday, underpinned by bullish dollar sentiment (ICE spot dollar index hit 11.5-year high 98.808 overnight, last 98.60 versus 97.79 early Tuesday). Daily chart positive-biased as stochastics bullish; MACD histogram bars turning positive; bullish parabolic stop-and-reverse signal hit Tuesday; 5- and 15-day moving averages advancing. Resistance at 1.2697 (Feb. 11 high); breach would expose upside to 1.2772 (Feb. 2 high), then to 1.2799 (near-six-year high hit Jan. 30) and the psychological 1.3000 line. Support at 1.2596 (Tuesday's low); breach would temper positive near-term view, targeting 1.2574 (Monday's low). Spot USD/CAD is at 1.2678. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0100 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Thailand's Finance Ministry\f28 \uc2 \f2  is confident the state investment budget disbursement will rise markedly in 2Q15 following earlier slow uses of allocated funds. Manas Jamveha, director general of the Comptroller General's Department at the finance ministry, says that the government's investment budget disbursement is expected to rise to 180 billion baht ($5.5 billion) or 40% of the total budget while all state offices will likely sign all procurement contracts in 2Q. "This has given us confidence that--throughout the current fiscal year--disbursement will hit 85% of the 449 billion baht (state) investment budget as planned," says Manas. Official record from the finance ministry shows about 17.9% of the government investment budget has been disbursed during October 2014, which was the start of the 2015 fiscal year, and February 2015.(nopparat.chaichalearmmongkol@wsj.com; Twitter: @nopbkk) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0059 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/CHF likely to consolidate with bullish bias Wednesday after hitting near-two-month high 1.0000 on Tuesday. Pair underpinned by bullish dollar sentiment (ICE spot dollar index hit 11.5-year high 98.808 overnight, last 98.59 versus 97.79 early Tuesday); negative Swiss interest rates; threat of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  CHF-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Daily chart positive-biased as MACD and stochastics bullish, although latter at overbought levels; five- and 15-day moving averages advancing. Rise above 1.0000 would expose upside to 1.0240 (Jan. 14 swing high), then to 1.0277 (Sept. 10, 2010 high) and 1.0626 (Aug. 11, 2010 high). Support at 0.9904 (hourly chart), then at 0.9850 (Tuesday's low); breach would temper positive near-term view, targeting 0.9817 (Monday's low). Spot USD/CHF is at 0.9979. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0058 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] GBP/USD likely to trade with risks skewed to the downside Wednesday. Pair undermined by bullish dollar sentiment (ICE spot dollar index hit 11.5-year high 98.808 overnight, last 98.63 versus 97.79 early Tuesday). But GBP/USD losses tempered by sterling demand on a soft EUR/GBP cross. Data focus Wednesday on 0930 GMT U.K. January industrial production (forecast +0.2% on-month, +1.3% on-year) and manufacturing output (forecast +0.2% on-month, +2.6% on-year). Daily chart negative-biased as MACD bearish, stochastics stays suppressed at oversold levels, five-day moving average below 15-day moving average and declining. Support at 1.5023 (Monday's low); breach would target 1.4986 (Feb. 3 low), then 1.4948 (one-and-a-half year low hit Jan. 23) and 1.4812 (July 9, 2013 swing low). Resistance at 1.5100 (hourly chart), then at 1.5136 (Monday's high); breach would temper negative near-term view, exposing upside to 1.5255 (Friday's high). Spot GBP/USD is at 1.5070. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0057 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Thailand's leader denies the military appointed government has been pressuring the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to lower the country's benchmark interest rate ahead of another monetary policy committee meeting. "Yes, we have discussed [the interest rate policy] during the cabinet meeting," says Prime Minister Prayuth Chan-ocha. "But we cannot signal anything because the Monetary Policy Committee is empowered and tasked to oversee the monetary measures. We cannot order anything." The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  has been keeping its policy rate at 2.0% since last March. But continued disinflation and negative inflation in the past two months along with Thailand's relatively strong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and weak exports have led private sectors and some economists to suggest a rate cut. Gen. Prayuth adds that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been keeping an eye on the situation to estimate how capital movement will affect the economy before making any decision.(nopparat.chaichalearmmongkol@wsj.com; Twitter: @nopbkk) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0047 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] NZD/USD likely to consolidate with bearish bias Wednesday after hitting one-month low 0.7249 on Tuesday as markets await 2000 GMT \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\f28 \uc2 \f2  interest rate announcement: RBNZ is widely expected to leave rates on hold at 3.5%. NZD/USD undermined by bullish dollar sentiment (ICE spot dollar index hit 11.5-year high 98.808 overnight, last 98.63 versus 97.79 early Tuesday); food safety issue for the New Zealand dairy industry after police revealed a threat to contaminate infant and other dairy formula. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD and stochastics bearish, although latter at oversold levels; five-day moving average below 15-day moving average and declining. Drop below 0.7249 would target 0.7174 (Feb. 3 low), then 0.7113 (March 17, 2011 reaction low) and the psychological 0.7000 line. Resistance at 0.7311 (hourly chart), then at 0.7359 (Tuesday's high); breach would temper negative near-term view, targeting 0.7390 (Monday's high). Spot NZD/USD is at 0.7273. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10 Mar 2015 21:32 ET \par Singapore Dollar Hits New Low as EUR Falls, MAS -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0041 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Japanese core machinery orders maintain their recovery trend despite a slight fall in January, says Junichi Makino, chief economist at SMBC Nikko Securities. "Following last month's large gains, the fall is very limited," he says. Core machinery orders fell 1.7% on month in January, after rising 8.3% in December. The figures are widely regarded as a leading indicator of capital expenditure, but are also known for their volatility. "Machinery orders are expected to continue recovering," Makino says. (alexander.martin@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March 10, 2015 21:32 ET (01:32 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150311eb3b0002a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
