\par\par \b \uc2 Outlook for Singapore: Less  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Appreciation, Less Yield -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,593 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 2015\par 09:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 0822 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] After the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week to lower the slope at which the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar appreciates, ING raises its end-of-year USD/SGD forecast above consensus to 1.41 from 1.36, while it lowers its forecast for the Singaporean 10-year government bond yield to 2.30% from 2.50% by year-end. The notes last yielded 1.99%. ING says yields on Singaporean sovereign debt should remain closely bound to its \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  counterparts as the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  starts to move, says ING. "Over the course of the US Fed's quantitative easing episodes the differential between US and Singapore government bond yields collapsed," the bank says.\par \par \uc2  "Pending a normalization of the Fed's balance sheet we expect this situation to persist and Singapore yields to move closely with US yields." (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @gregorhunter) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0819 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The ECB's decision to include inflation-linked debt in its upcoming government bond purchases was "a major surprise for the market," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which likes long positions in German and Italian linkers in breakeven terms, hedged with HICP inflation swaps. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2  says that German paper should see the largest amount of linker buying relative to the outstanding amount. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0759 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--\f28 \uc2 \f2 The Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  kept rates on hold and removed the 'considerable time' phase from its statement as expected, reiterated it could remain patient on rate hikes while at the same time upgrading its outlook for both the economy and the jobs market. That stance rattled stocks with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 S&P\f28 \uc2 \f2  shedding 1.4%, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow\f28 \uc2 \f2  had its lowest close of the year, while U.S. 10-yr yields fell to a fresh 20-month lows. In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  land the greenback firmed pretty much across the board, although it did give back a little ground in Asia. While the Fed is looking to raise rates at some time this year both the RBNZ and RBA are heading in the opposite direction with the former shifting to a neutral stance overnight, while the latter seems to be getting very close to a rate cut. AUD/USD and NZD/USD fell to respective fresh 5 and 4 year lows, while USD/CAD racked up another 5 year high as tumbling oil prices weighed down on the Loonie. Up ahead there's plenty of data on tap with German unemployment (0855GMT), EMU money supply (0900 GMT), EMU confidence surveys (1000 GMT) and the first look at German January HICP (1300 GMT.) gary.stride@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0758 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Cellphone penetration in Myanmar could now be as high as 15%, estimates Chris Tun, director at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deloitte Consulting\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Myanmar practice. Just four years ago, wireless penetration rates were only 3%, the lowest in the region. The boost, Tun says, is directly related to the entry of foreign operators \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ooredoo\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Telenor\f28 \uc2 \f2 , pushing down prices of SIM cards to just $1.50 from more than a hundred dollars. Still, cellphone services remain patchy and unreliable even in key cities like Yangon and Mandalay, as many new subscribers overload Myanmar's limited infrastructure. Confusion over land ownership and permits has slowed the process of building crucial telecom towers to support the new networks, and coverage in rural areas of the country remains limited.(shibani.mahtani@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0753 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Rate normalization will likely be a bigger influence on USD/SGD in coming months, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  says in a note after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore\f28 \uc2 \f2  puts the Singapore dollar on a gentler appreciation path. "In this regard, we expect the \f28 \uc2 \f2 US Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  to make its first rate hike around the second half of 2015 and see more broadening USD strength going forward," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. It predicts USD/SGD to be at 1.3700 in three months and 1.3900 in 12 months. The pair is currently at 1.3504. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0753 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Philippines's surprise 6.9% gross domestic product growth for the 2014 fourth-quarter isn't enough for economists at Capital Economics to revise up their 2015 GDP forecast for the Southeast Asian country. The research group said its forecast of 6.5% GDP growth remains unchanged despite the country's improved business environment, low interest rates and a healthy export performance. "This is slightly above the consensus, which is expecting growth of 6.2% in 2015," Gareth Leather, Asia economist at Capital Economics said in a note. Capital Economics' outlook, like most other forecasts, falls short of the Philippine government's target of 7%-8% growth this year. (cris.larano@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: FLS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0750 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Philippines's surprise 6.9% gross domestic product growth for the 2014 fourth-quarter isn't enough for economists at Capital Economics to revise up their 2015 GDP forecast for the Southeast Asian country. The research group said its forecast of 6.5% GDP growth remains unchanged despite the country's improved business environment, low interest rates and a healthy export performance. "This is slightly above the consensus, which is expecting growth of 6.2% in 2015," Gareth Leather, Asia economist at Capital Economics said in a note. Capital Economics' outlook, like most other forecasts, falls short of the Philippine government's target of 7%-8% growth this year. (cris.larano@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: FLS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0752 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The newly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  oil pipeline running across Myanmar to China, carrying oil from the Bay of Bengal to Yunnan province, will likely be under-utilized in the first years of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , says Yun Sun, an expert on China-Myanmar relations at the Washington, D.C.-based Stimson Center. The pipeline began \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Wednesday, more than six months after the scheduled date. Yun Sun says the delay was due to slow construction of a deep-sea port in Myanmar, which was not ready to handle big oil tankers till this January. The 771-kilometre long pipeline can transmit up to 22 million tonnes of oil per year, allowing China to bypass the Straits of Malacca and ship crucial energy resources directly from the Bay of Bengal to its southwestern provinces. (shibani.mahtani@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0737 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Copper prices are trading close to a five-and-half-year low in late Asia trade, briefly touching the level before edging higher. Concerns over weaker demand from China and fears of deflation due to plunging crude oil prices have clouded the industrial metal's outlook. At 0722 GMT, three-month copper future prices on the London Metal Exchange were at $5,417 per ton, rebounding from the low of $5,405/ton.(biman.mukherji@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: DWT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0740 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Peripheral bond markets are expected to claw back some of Wednesday's losses with the upcoming German inflation data highlighting that the ECB is facing a challenge in lifting inflation expectations back to the targeted path, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Says that today's 5-yr and 10-yr BTP auctions should be easily absorbed with investors also "front-running" the ECB before buying begins in March. "With coupon and redemptions payments worth EUR60bn looming we expect large peripherals to find a firmer footing again." In the 10-yr space, Germany yield 0.34%, Italy 1.62% and Spain 1.46%. (nick.cawley@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0740 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Bank of England Gov. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mark Carney\f28 \uc2 \f2  says building a eurozone fiscal union would be a good thing for rich member states like Germany and not just the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  area's poorer members. In an interview with the Irish Times, Mr. Carney builds on a speech he delivered in Dublin Wednesday calling for transfers of public cash within the 19-nation bloc to ameliorate downturns in the region's economy. "Building greater private risk-sharing and greater public risk-sharing are as much in the interest of the German people as they are of any constituent part of the eurozone," he is quoted as saying. His remarks are likely to cut little ice in Berlin, where fiscal transfers are politically toxic. (jason.douglas@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0738 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--South Africa's President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jacob Zuma\f28 \uc2 \f2  is unlikely to make changes that would inspire investors to return to the continent's most developed economy during the final four years of his presidential tenure, Barclays Africa economist Peter Worthington said Thursday. South Africa has struggled for years to reassure investors and jumpstart economic growth much above the current rate of around 1.5% annually. It might take a change of government to draw investors in numbers sufficient enough to change that, Mr. Worthington said. "When we do get a different administration with different leadership at the top, maybe there could be a kick to investor confidence that could bring in investment again," he said. patrick.mcgroarty@wsj.com. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 29, 2015 03:22 ET (08:22 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150129eb1t000x3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
