\par\par \b \uc2 Outlook for Singapore: Less  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Appreciation, Less Yield -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 176 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 2015\par 09:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 0822 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] After the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week to lower the slope at which the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar appreciates, ING raises its end-of-year USD/SGD forecast above consensus to 1.41 from 1.36, while it lowers its forecast for the Singaporean 10-year government bond yield to 2.30% from 2.50% by year-end. The notes last yielded 1.99%. ING says yields on Singaporean sovereign debt should remain closely bound to its \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  counterparts as the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  starts to move, says ING. "Over the course of the US Fed's quantitative easing episodes the differential between US and Singapore government bond yields collapsed," the bank says.\par \par \uc2  "Pending a normalization of the Fed's balance sheet we expect this situation to persist and Singapore yields to move closely with US yields." (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; Twitter: @gregorhunter) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 29, 2015 03:22 ET (08:22 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150129eb1t0010m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
