\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Bullish Fed hurts Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 ; Singapore dlr rebounds\b0\par\par\uc2 868 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 2015\par 07:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Ringgit falls on low oil prices concerns\par     * Won around 4-week low on offshore selling\par     * \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dlr rises on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  caution; outlook dark\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SEOUL, Jan 29 (Reuters) - Most emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par fell on Thursday as the U.S. Federal Reserve appeared on track\par to raise interest rates this year while there are risks that\par regional central banks instead may ease monetary policies.\par     The Malaysian ringgit hit a near six-year low as an\par overnight slide in oil prices highlighted concerns that slumping\par crude will hurt the country's current account surplus and\par increase its fiscal deficit.\par     South Korea's won touched a near four-week low as\par offshore funds sold it, while Taiwan's dollar slid on\par short-covering in the U.S. dollar.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar, however, rebounded on caution\par over potential \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support it.\par Still, its outlook stayed pessimistic after Wednesday's\par unexpected monetary policy easing. \par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  said on Wednesday the U.S. economy was expanding "at\par a solid pace" with strong job gains, signalling that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remains on track with its plans to hike borrowing costs in\par 2015. \par     "With the Fed's healthy economic assessment, Asian\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may stay under pressure for the time being," said\par Yuna Park, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and bond analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dongbu Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  in\par Seoul.\par     "Asian countries may take similar policy stances with\par Singapore's move through driving their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weaker," she\par said. \par     Prior to the Fed statement, emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had\par been supported, partially due to some expectations it might take\par a dovish stance at a time other central banks have eased policy\par amid fears of deflation.\par         \par     RINGGIT\par     The ringgit fell 0.5 percent to 3.6350 per dollar, its\par weakest since April 2009.\par     The Malaysian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's depreciation accelerated as a break\par of the previous low of 3.6250 hit on Jan. 21 spurred selling,\par traders said.\par     The unit pared some of its losses on caution over possible\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Traders largely ignored the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's decision on\par Wednesday to keep interest rates unchanged, which was expected.\par  \par     \par     WON\par     The won lost as much as 0.9 percent to 1,094.7 per dollar,\par its weakest since Jan. 9.\par     Offshore funds' offers caused local traders to cut their won\par holdings.\par     Some traders dumped bullish bets on the won, which they had\par built up on expectations of more demand from exporters for\par month-end settlements.\par     \par     TAIWAN DOLLAR\par     The Taiwan dollar slid as traders covered short positions in\par the U.S. dollar after the Fed's statement.\par     Importers also bought the greenback for payments, while\par exporters stayed away on expectations of further strength in the\par U.S. dollar.\par     Traders remained cautious over possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the\par island's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , saying that near the local market closing\par time on previous sessions, the authority was spotted selling the\par Taiwan dollar to stem its strength.\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  DOLLAR\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar gained amid caution over possible\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support it after a surprise\par easing sent the unit to a near four-and-a-half year low on\par Wednesday.\par     Still, the city-state \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expected to remain weak,\par analysts and traders said.\par     "In a nutshell, expect the latest policy easing to continue\par to put the hurt on the SGD, keeping the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on lockdown as\par a relative underperformer in the region," said Emmanuel Ng, a\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist with \f28 \uc2 \f2 OCBC Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 , in a client note.\par     Economists revised downward their forecasts for the\par Singapore dollar.\par     Edward Teather, a senior economist with \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2 , said the\par investment bank revised its end-2015 forecast for the Singapore\par dollar's value against the greenback to 1.4000.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tim Condon\f28 \uc2 \f2 , head of Asia research at ING, also said it\par changed its year-end forecast to 1.4100 from 1.3600.\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0630 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       117.66         117.58       -0.07\par   Sing dlr        1.3510         1.3532       +0.16\par   Taiwan dlr      31.410         31.328       -0.26\par   Korean won     1092.75        1084.50       -0.75\par   Baht             32.60          32.53       -0.21\par   Peso             44.16          44.09       -0.17\par   Rupiah        12520.00       12485.00       -0.28\par   Rupee            61.50          61.41       -0.15\par   Ringgit         3.6300         3.6185       -0.32\par   Yuan            6.2490         6.2480       -0.02\par  \par   Change so far in 2015\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       117.66         119.66       +1.70\par   Sing dlr        1.3510         1.3260       -1.85\par   Taiwan dlr      31.410         31.718       +0.98\par   Korean won     1092.75        1099.30       +0.60\par   Baht             32.60          32.90       +0.93\par   Peso             44.16          44.72       +1.27\par   Rupiah        12520.00       12380.00       -1.12\par   Rupee            61.50          63.03       +2.49\par   Ringgit         3.6300         3.4965       -3.68\par   Yuan            6.2490         6.2040       -0.72\par  \par  (Additional reporting by Jeanny Kao in TAIPEI, Vidya\par Ranganathan in SINGAPORE and IFR Markets' Catherine Tan; Editing\par by Richard Borsuk)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-1-29T07:34:00.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument