\par\par \b \uc2 BREAKINGVIEWS- \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  market still scarred by Swiss trauma\b0\par\par\uc2 610 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2015\par 11:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (The author is a Reuters Breakingviews columnist. The opinions expressed are her own.)\par \par \uc2 By Swaha Pattanaik\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 28 (Reuters Breakingviews) - On the surface, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading has recovered from brutal swings triggered nearly two weeks ago when the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  suddenly abandoned its franc cap. But look a little deeper, and there are still signs of trauma. A surprise policy easing from Singapore on Jan. 28 will add to the market's unease.\par \par \uc2 For the franc, post-shock liquidity remains patchy. There has been a widening of the bid-offer spread, the gap between the price at which traders are willing to buy the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the one at which they will sell. Against the dollar, this spread was 10 times wider during the past few trading days than its 2014 average, \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  strategists say.\par \par \uc2 No wonder. Many dealers were burned when the SNB jettisoned its cap on Jan. 15 and the franc rocketed by as much as 40 percent against the euro. Even banks which managed not to lose money in the biggest intra-day \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  move in living memory will be more cautious.\par \par \uc2 There have also been some perplexing price moves. The euro gained 2 percent against the Swiss franc during European morning trading on Jan. 27, only to cede most its gains within a couple of hours. Traders scrambled for an explanation. Perhaps there was an unwinding of short euro positions or maybe the SNB was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The twitchiness is understandable: there is high uncertainty about what Swiss policymakers are up to. Rising cash balances in the commercial banks' accounts at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suggest the SNB has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cap gone, traders are in the dark about the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tactics and strategy.\par \par \uc2 The Singapore example could be pertinent. An SNB official has said that the city-state's policy of keeping the Singapore dollar within an undisclosed trading band against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  "deserved closer examination." As Wednesday's move by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore\uc2  shows, this approach can also invite surprises.\par \par \uc2 If Switzerland is anything to go by, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders are going to have to get used to a more ambiguous world.\par \par \uc2 \f2               \par               \par               \par               \par CONTEXT NEWS\par \par               \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Singapore's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  unexpectedly eased monetary policy on Jan. 28 by reducing the slope of its policy band for the Singapore dollar. It said the inflation outlook had "shifted significantly" since its last review in October 2014.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Swiss National Bank Vice Chairman \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jean-Pierre Danthine\f28 \uc2 \f2  told the TagesAnzeiger daily in an interview published on Jan. 27 that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was ready to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to ease monetary policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Asked about a new \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy, Danthine said Denmark's policy of pegging the crown to the euro would not be suitable for Switzerland, but that Singapore's system, which allows the Singapore dollar to rise or fall against the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of its main trading partners within an undisclosed trading band, "deserved closer examination."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - SNB data on Jan. 26 showed that the amount of cash commercial banks hold with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rose the most since at least March 2013, suggesting the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to keep the franc down despite scrapping a cap against the euro.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Reuters: Swiss sight deposits surge after SNB abandons franc cap\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Reuters: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  says stands ready to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Reuters: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  unexpectedly eases monetary policy as oil plunge cools inflation\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RELATED COLUMN\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Foundering faith\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - For previous columns by the author, Reuters customers can click on\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Editing by Edward Hadas and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sarah Bailey\f28 \uc2 \f2 )\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-1-31T10:07:45.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150128eb1s00c9p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
