\par\par \b \uc2 USD/TWD Falls; 30.480 Floor Tipped - Trader -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,858 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 03:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0234 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD falls sharply, tracking the USD/KRW and USD/CNY lower following weak U.S. data including a fall in manufacturing PMI and a Fed official's comments suggesting interest rates won't be raised till 2H 2015, says a local trader. The pair is at 30.500, lower than 30.573 traded before the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday, which lifted it to 30.622 at the close of onshore trading session, traders say. "Foreign investors are selling actively...the greenback is still likely to decline further after falling below NT$30.500," a trader says, and tips the pair to trade in a 30.480-30.530 band for the session. Foreign investors were net buyers of NT$7.13 billion worth of local shares Monday, shrugging off domestic tensions. The police early Monday morning removed protesters who stormed the cabinet building on Sunday evening as the government rejected their demands to retract a services trade agreement with China. (fanny.liu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0223 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude-oil futures are lower in early Asian trade Tuesday with Nymex WTI futures extending overnight losses. Brent crude also slipped from its previous close. "The disruption in supply that was expected due to the Russian situation, is not happening. Prices will rise if there are some strong sanctions against Russia," says Gnanasekhar Thiagarajan, director of Commtrendz Risk Management. The sanctions so far are not strong enough to disrupt supplies, he says. President Barack Obama last Thursday expanded the sanctions on 20 Russians and a St. Petersburg bank; Russia responded with its own sanctions against six U.S. lawmakers and three White House officials. "Overall, the trend is bearish. U.S. which is one of the largest consumers, has huge stockpiles. Its shale inventories are also rising," he says. At 0147 GMT, Nymex WTI for May delivery is at $99.29/bbl, down 31 cents and May Brent crude is at $106.51/bbl, down 30 cents from its previous close. (biman.mukherji@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0216 GMT [Dow Jones] The Aussie/yen cross retreated to as low as 93.35 from its morning high of 93.58 in Asian trade Tuesday as Tokyo stocks struggled in negative territory. Japan's Nikkei Stock Average was recently down 0.34% at 14,426.22. Further downside in the Aussie/yen cross may encounter intraday support at 93.21 and 93.09. Spot AUD/JPY was recently at 93.42 compared with 93.32 in late New York trade Tuesday. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0216 GMT [Dow Jones] All that talk about China rolling out stimulus measures to head off its weakening growth? It's nothing but wishful thinking, says Reorient Financial Markets. Stimulus is "neither likely nor necessary," economists say, noting China has of late rolled out reform after reform, including the recent widening of the yuan trading band and introduction of a preferred share program for some mainland companies. "To repeat, such overall reform measures ARE the stimulus," economists say. "China's new leaders have proved on several occasions that they will not be nervously diverted from their course of action by minor setbacks." Reality looks to be sinking in in Hong Kong--the Hang Seng China Enterprises Index is down 0.5% in early trade after rallying 2.8% Monday. (mia.lamar@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0209 GMT [Dow Jones] The Singapore dollar which touched 1.2669 versus the U.S. dollar has now retreated slightly to consolidate near the USD/SGD Ichimoku Cloud technical support at 1.2672. If the USD/SGD drops under the Ichimoku Cloud--which bottoms out at 1.2647--it would also be within the Bollinger downtrend channel that could pull the U.S. dollar even lower in the near term. The greenback has fallen broadly on a toning down of hawkish rhetoric from the U.S. Federal Reserve. Reassurance from San Francisco Federal Reserve President John Williams that interest rates will not be raised till the second half of 2015 took a toll on the U.S. dollar overnight. Coupled with market chatter of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent days to buffer the Singapore dollar, USD/SGD topside is likely to be very limited for now. USD/SGD is now 1.2679 from its Monday close of 1.2709. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0204 GMT [Dow Jones] The Indonesian rupiah is stronger Tuesday at 11,375 to the U.S. dollar compared to its Monday close of 11,420, and may rally further. USD/IDR is likely to carry on its bearish path toward 11,160 now that expectations for the U.S. Federal Reserve to raise interest rates sooner have been dampened. Overnight, the U.S. dollar index subsided after San Francisco Federal Reserve President John Williams reassured markets that U.S. interest rates will not be raised till H2 2105. Now, investors will likely re-focus on the June presidential elections that will likely boost appeal for Indonesian assets. Historically, Indonesian stocks have mostly rallied in election years. The market is optimistic for presidential candidate Joko Widodo to win--which would be a boost for foreign investor sentiment, and could therefore, help the rupiah to appreciate. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0154 GMT [Dow Jones] The Taiwan dollar recovers from Monday's 2.5-year low of 30.667 to the U.S. dollar and may next head for the base of the daily Bollinger uptrend channel at 30.460. The drop in USD/TWD follows the overnight fall of the U.S. dollar after \uc2 San Francisco Federal Reserve Bank\uc2  President John Williams said monetary policy remains fundamentally unchanged and reiterating that interest rates won't rise till H2 2015. The reassurance from the Fed had a kneejerk effect of weakening the U.S. dollar against the majors, but continues to influence USD/Asia lower Tuesday. If USD/TWD stays above 30.460 by the end of the day, the near-term technical upside bias for the greenback may remain in effect. USD/TWD is now 30.505 from its Monday close of 30.622. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0149 GMT [Dow Jones] The flushing out of long-USD/KRW positions on Monday continues today, fuelled by slightly bearish dollar comments from San Francisco Federal Reserve President John Williams. The Fed official said U.S. interest rates won't be raised till H2 2015, which helped to calm skittish investors who have been buying back dollar-shorts since the Fed's policy meeting last Wednesday. USD/KRW could retreat toward technical support at the base of the daily Bollinger uptrend channel at 1,074 and then consolidate. However, if this support is upheld by the end of the day, the near-term bias for the U.S. dollar will stay bullish. Mild weakness in the South Korean stock market, which is down 0.2%, could also keep won strength at bay. USD/KRW is now 1,076.8 from its Monday close of 1,077.8. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0146 GMT [Dow Jones] China's yuan is up for a second day after the overnight fall of the U.S. dollar index and the sharp drop in USD/CNY on Monday, which implies the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is no longer actively weakening the yuan. Spot USD/CNY could next slip to the base of the daily Bollinger uptrend channel at 6.1500, which is also a round-figure trading barrier that will add to support for the dollar. The poor China \uc2 HSBC\uc2  flash manufacturing PMI for March released on Monday has had little adverse impact on investor sentiment. The consensus view appears to be that the Chinese government might implement stimulus measures to ensure that it can still meet its 2014 economic growth target of 7.5%. The reduced bearishness for the yuan can also be seen in the offshore nondeliverable forward market, where the price gap between the 1-month NDF contract and the 12-month contract has narrowed, implying that traders are less bearish on the yuan's value over a 12-month period. Spot USD/CNY is now 6.1824 from its Monday close of 6.1888. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0133 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold edges higher in early Asia trade on modest dip-buying after prices fall 1.9% in the spot market overnight. While safe-haven appeal in gold has dimmed as tensions in Ukraine shows signs of easing, expectations of higher interest rates in the U.S. have further dented gold's demand. The prospect of interest rates in the U.S. rising soon may have "dampened" the inflation disaster scenario for some gold bulls with regard to the after-effects of the U.S. ultra-easy money policy, Scott Schuberg, chief executive of Rivkin Securities Pty Ltd, says in a report. Gold will be "much better assessed using technical analysis than attempting to justify the effect of changing fundamental factors," he says. Spot gold is at $1,311.30/oz, up $1.70 from its previous close. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0128 GMT [Dow Jones] Some semblance of stability appears to be returning to the Chinese yuan, DBS says, after the parity rate was fixed lower at 6.1452 on Monday and 6.1426 on Tuesday compared with 6.1475 on Friday, and the yuan strengthened on hopes that China might stimulate its economy. The bank notes that U.S. President Barack Obama urged Chinese President Xi Jinping to move the yuan toward a more flexible and market-based exchange rate in spite of Beijing's latest decision on March 15 to double the USD/CNY trading band to 2% around parity. That suggests the yuan's recent slide may halt ahead of the upcoming \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Report scheduled for release in April, DBS says. The offshore spot USD/CNH was recently at 6.1795 compared with 6.1799 late Monday. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 24 Mar 2014 22:34 EDT  USD/TWD Falls; 30.480 Floor Tipped - Trader -- -2-\par \par \uc2 0108 GMT [Dow Jones] Although U.K. February CPI (due at 0930 GMT Tuesday) is expected to come in at +1.7% on-year, declining further below the Bank of England's 2.0% target, the pound is likely to hold up well, \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  says. The bank thinks markets are unlikely roll back BOE rate-hike expectations in response to the expected soft CPI data and notes that long-GBP positioning has been substantially reduced. The sterling/dollar pair fell 2.15% from 1.6822 Feb. 17 to a low of 1.6459 Monday. \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  thinks risks for sterling are to the upside heading into the second quarter this year. Spot GBP/USD was recently at 1.6500 compared with 1.6496 in late New York trade Monday. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 24, 2014 22:34 ET (02:34 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140325ea3p000e1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
