\par\par \b \uc2 USD/SGD Down But Not Out; 1.2717 Support Eyed -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,702 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 05:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0448 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD has fallen over the last two days but will retain its bullish technical bias--implying the Singapore dollar could weaken--if it ends Monday inside the daily Bollinger uptrend channel. The bullish technical signal depends on USD/SGD closing above 1.2717 today. USD/SGD slipped as the Singapore dollar rebound from losses sustained when the U.S. unit rose sharply last week after the U.S. Federal Reserve policy meeting. The strengthening of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar occurred amid market chatter of possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Friday by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Though unconfirmed, the possibility of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore\f28 \uc2 \f2  supporting the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is a factor that will likely keep USD/SGD capped for now. USD/SGD is now 1.2721 from its Friday close of 1.2739. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0437 GMT [Dow Jones] The Australian dollar is trading with a weaker tone against its New Zealand counterpart in Asia, retreating from a nine-day high of 1.0659 set early this morning to a low of 1.0613, after \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's flash manufacturing PMI report for China came in weak. Australia's biggest export market disappointed with the PMI falling to 48.1 in March from a final February reading of 48.5. Further downside in spot AUD/NZD may encounter intraday support at 1.0610, and then at 1.0593. Spot AUD/NZD was recently at 1.0628 compared with 1.0631 in New York late Friday. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0431 GMT [Dow Jones] The Aussie/yen cross is trading with a buoyant tone in Asian trade Monday, climbing to as high as 93.06 from 92.81 in New York late Friday, as rising Tokyo stocks on bargain hunting overshadowed the weak preliminary HSBC China manufacturing PMI data for March. Japan's Nikkei Stock Average was recently up 1.95% at 14,501.64. Further upside in the Aussie/yen cross may encounter resistance at 93.08 and 93.40. Spot AUD/JPY was recently at 93.00. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0333 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY falls as the central parity is set lower to reflect the dollar's weakness overseas. The pair is at 6.2185 vs 6.2250 Friday close, after the central parity is set at 6.1452 vs 6.1475 Friday. But traders say the yuan may remain weak for a while after the preliminary HSBC China PMI fell to an eight-month low of 48.1 in March, indicating further contraction in manufacturing activities. "The yuan is simply taking a rest. Investors still think the PBOC wants a weaker yuan for now and data gives more signal on growth slowdown," says a Shanghai-based local bank trader. The yuan lost 1.2% last week after the PBOC announced a trading band widening for the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0326 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD dipped after the preliminary HSBC China Manufacturing Purchasing Managers Index, a gauge of nationwide manufacturing activity, fell to an eight-month low of 48.1 in March, from a final reading of 48.5 in February. The pair is at 0.8532 versus 0.8549 early in New Zealand. "There are continued concerns over our major export market. If they slow down, obviously it is very bad for the New Zealand economy," Sam Tuck, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at ANZ, says. He notes the data showed a minor downtick but "it is the reason why the Kiwi is on the defensive at the moment." Looking ahead, he says markets will be watching for flash PMIs out of Europe and the U.S. later in the global trading day. He puts support for the Kiwi at 0.8480 to 0.8500 and resistance at 0.8560 to 0.8570. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 0326 GMT [Dow Jones] Wednesday's Financial Stability Review from the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  should attract more attention than usual, given that the housing market is the one pocket of the economy where low interest rates are getting significant traction, say \uc2 J.P.Morgan\uc2  economists. "Given recent noises around the potential broadening of the RBA's suite of tools to create a more balanced growth path, further musings on the use of macro-prudential policy in the FSR (following last week's mention in the March Board minutes) would add to the sense that rate hikes remain a distant prospect," they say. (david.rogers@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0322 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei is up 1.8% at 14,484.06 midday on reasonably good volume (1.4 billion shares), as bargain-hunting and some short covering more than offset preliminary HSBC China purchasers manufacturing index (PMI) figures showing that activity continued to fall in March. The data hit an eight-month low of 48.1 in March from 48.5 in February, missing expectations. A reading below 50 reflects contraction, while above 50 shows expansion. But \uc2 HSBC\uc2  also said that it expects Beijing to launch a series of policy measures to stabilize growth, which may have had a mollifying effect. "The whole of Asia seems to have factored in lowered expectations for China's growth," says Yoshihiro Okumura, general manager at Chibagin Asset Management. "Traders unsure of how the added tax [in Japan] will affect consumer spending and corporate profits have been large sellers in recent sessions, but bargain-hunters are coming out today." The Nikkei fell to a six-week low last Thursday, before Friday's market holiday. Among major gainers, Sharp (6753.TO) is up 5.3% at Y296, while Nintendo (7974.TO) is up 6.5% at Y12,295. Yamato Holdings (9064.TO)\par \par \uc2 0318 GMT [Dow Jones] March's deterioration in China's manufacturing data puts the prospect of looser economic policy on the table for the next six months, says \uc2 PNC Financial Services Group\uc2 , a Pittsburgh-based bank with $127bn in assets under management. "Just as tight credit and higher interest rates in the second half of 2013 translated into slower growth in the first quarter of 2014, looser credit and government support for investment in early 2014 will support growth later this year," says Bill Adams, senior international economist at PNC. "China's economic policy stance is data dependent. March's weaker data make policy likely to loosen at the margin in the second and third quarters of 2014." \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's China flash manufacturing PMI for March fell to an eight-month low of 48.1 compared with expectations of 48.7. Markets pared earlier gains on the data release but remain higher than their Friday close, with the Hang Seng Index up 0.9% at 21,6358.17. (gregor.hunter@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0314 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore's CPI inflation rate likely rose 0.8% on year in February compared with 1.4% in January, according to the median estimate in a poll of five analysts by The Wall Street Journal. Measured on-month in seasonally adjusted terms, the consumer price index likely rose 0.2%, according to the median of three estimates in the same poll. Data due 0500 GMT Monday. (venkat.pr@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0304 GMT [Dow Jones] The 'flash' purchasing managers' index released this morning confirms that China's economy has slowed significantly in the last three months, says Chen Xingdong, an economist at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  in Beijing. "Based on this, the first quarter's growth should be pretty weak," he says. The official growth figure is unlikely to be any higher than 7.1% or 7.2% on-year, he believes, compared with 7.7% in the final quarter of 2013. The 'flash' or preliminary reading of the HSBC Manufacturing PMI came in at 48.1 in March, down from 48.5 in February, where any number below 50 indicates contraction. Industrial output grew at 8.6% on-year in the first two months of 2014, the slowest pace in five years. Taken together, the data indicate that manufacturing expanded by less than 7% on-year, although services and construction could help lift the overall growth figure a little, says Chen. (richard.silk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0258 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD dipped from 0.9083 to 0.9049 after HBSC's disappointing China flash manufacturing PMI added to offshore macro headwinds capping rallies above 0.9100. The PMI's fall to 48.1 in March from a final February reading of 48.5 suggests there's some underlying weakness apart from Lunar New Year effects. The data are consistent with 7.4% GDP growth in China this year, says TD's head of Asia-Pacific Annette Beacher, but the downward direction of this indicator may add to caution about China's economy. Together with patchy U.S. economic data and lingering geopolitical risks surrounding Russia's increasing control over Ukraine, the PMI data offsets the recent positive influence of the RBA's recent move to a neutral policy stance. AUD/USD has strong technical resistance in the 0.9080-0.9140 area. AUD/USD last 0.9072. (david.rogers@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0244 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB is rising toward the upper end of its technical range of 32.22-32.50, implying a weaker baht, as political uncertainty returns. Thailand investors are likely anxious after Thailand's Constitutional Court on Friday ruled that the general election held on February 2 was invalid. Political tension and violence is likely to return, which would be negative for the Thailand economy and thus for the baht. USD/THB is currently caught in a technical range demarcated by the lower and upper Bollinger bands (standard deviation 1). The daily and weekly USD/THB technical charts are both lacking directional cues. Unless the dollar breaks the 32.22-32.50 range, USD/THB may meander for a while longer. USD/THB is now 32.43 from its Friday close of 32.36. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 24, 2014 00:50 ET (04:50 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140324ea3o000e5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
