\par\par \b \uc2 Ringgit Hits Four-Year Low but  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Caps Losses -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,731 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 2014\par 02:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0138 GMT [Dow Jones] The Malaysia ringgit is weaker for a third day as risk aversion sweeps across all asset classes sending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a fresh four-year low of 3.3455. But further losses may be limited by suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  near 3.3470, one onshore trader says. The dollar is, however, likely to remain well supported as investors look to scuttle positions that were short USD. The USD/MYR nondeliverable forward contract, which trades in the offshore market, indicates very strong demand for dollars. The price premium the one-month USD/MYR contract commands over the spot contract price has increased significantly--a sign of panicky short-USD covering. The tight control over the onshore value of the ringgit is turning speculative traders toward the NDF market, which is unregulated, thus fueling the spike in the NDF contract premium. USD/MYR is now 3.3430 from its Friday close of 3.3330. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0123 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude-oil futures are mixed in early Asian trade narrowing the Brent-WTI spread to under $11 a barrel. Nymex WTI crude gained over 2% last week on higher fuel demand due to extreme cold, bullish agency forecasts and the start of Transcanada's oil pipeline; offsetting a decline in financial markets on concerns about emerging market growth. \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says the impact of severe winter weather is likely to be a one-off and the market's focus will return to the impact of refinery maintenance in the first half of this year. "Strong U.S. refining margins and low product stock cover in the OECD could push WTI prices even higher in the coming weeks as the U.S. continues exporting high levels of light products," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  analyst Michael Cohen says. "We expect the [Brent-WTI] spread to continue to compress in the coming months, but a widening is possible during the peaks of the turnaround seasons in the U.S. and Europe," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says. Nymex crude is up 26 cents at $96.90/bbl, Brent is down 15 cents at $107.73/bbl. (eric.yep@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0122 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW gaps much higher at Monday's opening of 1,085.5 versus Friday's close of 1,080.4 as stop-loss orders triggered above 1,080 send the dollar up. The fear of a mass liquidation of risk-on positions has been driving most USD/Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs higher since late last week. The South Korea won has fallen as much as 1.2% since Thursday's closing. As signs of world-wide risk aversion materialize more investors are likely to dump Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the perceived safety of the U.S. dollar. Stock markets in Asia are down in line with Wall Street's losses on Friday with the Nikkei falling 2.7% and the Kospi dropping 1.8% so far--adding to already shaky investor sentiment. USD/KRW is now at 1,085.8 from its Friday close of 1,080.4. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0121 GMT [Dow Jones] Though the technical upside bias for USD/SGD remains in effect, further gains for the greenback may be less pronounced versus the Singapore dollar. The Singapore dollar is recognized by most investors as a regional safe haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , due to the country's strong economic fundamentals, and thus tends to hold its value better in times of risk aversion. Traders are currently looking at relative-value trades within Asia that entail betting on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Singapore dollar to do well against regional peers. That said, the greenback could still grind higher as long as the greenback stays above 1.2770 and thereby within the Bollinger uptrend channel. USD/SGD could notch a new 5-month peak if it surpasses the 1.2859 record last seen in August. USD/SGD is now at 1.2781 from its Friday close of 1.2785. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0058 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Graeme Wheeler will likely remain on hold Thursday "simply because his last set of forecasts indicated a first hike would not be until March/April, if not June, and the net weight of evidence has not been enough to swing the Bank's mind to a definite January" move, says Craig Ebert, senior economist at \uc2 Bank of New Zealand\uc2 . He adds, however, he would be "most disappointed and critical" if Wheeler fails to strongly signal a rate hike in March and the upside potential for the cash rate more generally. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 0039 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Barclays\uc2  has a "bearish view" on copper after prices fell below $7,210/ton last week. LME 3-month copper ended 0.3% lower Friday at $7,180/ton, adding to Thursday's 1.2% fall. \uc2 Barclays\uc2 ' target for the red metal is near $7,120/ton and then the $7,040/ton area, based on technical analysis. Base metals in general are likely to remain under pressure after weak manufacturing data from China last week--China is the world's biggest consumer of industrial metals--and the biggest loss for U.S. stocks in more than seven months Friday. Nickel, which was being supported until recently by a ban on unprocessed ore from Indonesia, fell 1.2% to $14,485/ton Friday. Trading activity is expected to remain light as Chinese traders continue to wind down ahead of their week-long Lunar New Year holiday, that starts on Jan. 31. Weak equity markets, which tend to reflect risk sentiment in the investment community, could also weigh on base metal prices. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0039 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's trade balance for December saw a deficit of Y1.3 trillion, bigger than expected, and large trade deficits are likely to continue, says \uc2 Meiji Yasuda Life Insurance\uc2  chief economist Yuichi Kodama. "Exports to emerging economies are slow," he says. "Even though the yen has weakened, the amount of exports hasn't increased much. The price of goods exported from Japan denominated in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has barely fallen." He said Japanese exporters didn't raise the prices of their goods even when the Japanese yen strengthened to record levels a couple of years ago, which eroded their profit margins. So even when the yen weakened in recent months, "there isn't much room for lowering the prices" as they are busy recovering lost margins, he says. "Contrary to expectations held by many, exports are unlikely to lead the economy," he says.(kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0015 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand's January cash rate decision is "likely to be finely balanced," but \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2  expects the outcome to be a 25bp rise in the official cash rate. It notes "inflation is rising and is now closer to the mid-point of the policy band than the bottom of it, and the RBNZ is now explicitly targeting the mid-point." While the NZD is high and recent market volatility across some emerging market economies is a "timely reminder we still face challenges," those challenges are "simply being usurped by a considerable margin by the positives, which are many," it says. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 2351 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to raise rates by 25 basis points this week to 2.75% given an economy at the "beginning of a boom" and inflation and activity outstripping RBNZ forecasts, Paul Bloxham, \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's Australia and New Zealand chief economist, says. With the economy at capacity and demand strong, "it is clear that rates should be lifted soon to keep inflation in check," he says. Money markets are currently pricing in a 50% chance of a rate hike Thursday. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 2044 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZX-50 will likely open in the red after U.S. markets "took a big smack" overnight Friday, says Forsyth Barr Investment Advisor Peter Young. U.S. stocks finished the week with deep losses as investors fled equities and emerging-markets \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on concerns about a contagion effect from China's manufacturing slowdown. Young notes that holidays in Australia and also in New Zealand's largest city of Auckland will likely keep volumes low on the NZX-50, "but it is fair to say the market will likely be down across the board." The NZX-50 closed 0.8% lower Friday at 4873.70. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @farrohoward)\par \par \uc2 2031 GMT [Dow Jones] Minutes from the BoJ's December meeting are unlikely to give the USD/JPY any relief, says BNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Kymberly Martin. In the past few days JPY has been sought for its safe-haven status regardless of the market's expectations about BoJ policy and any further stimulus, and "it will take some improvement in sentiment toward risk and emerging markets for JPY weakness to once again take hold," she says. The pair is at 102.412 early in New Zealand. Martin tips support at 102.00 and resistance at 103.40. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @farrohoward)\par \par \uc2 1938 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD and AUD/USD remain under pressure on jitters about emerging markets and expectations the Fed might reduce monthly bond purchases again this week, "but they aren't sinking like stones," says Stuart Ive, Wellington-based senior client adviser for OM Financial. He expects a fairly quiet session given the Australia Day holiday across the Tasman, and says both pairs may see some consolidation ahead of the FOMC and then the RBNZ's rate decision later in the week. While the chances of a potential rate hike in New Zealand Thursday have increased due to positive local data and higher-than-expected inflation, Ive says the events of late last week--including the sell-off in emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 --may see the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  opt to stay on hold. The NZD/USD is at 0.8234 early in New Zealand, versus 0.8285 late Friday, while the AUD/USD is at 0.8698 versus 0.8760. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @farrohoward)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 26, 2014 20:39 ET (01:39 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140127ea1r0001a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
