\par\par \b \uc2 Southeast Asia Ends 2013 Mixed; Vietnam Leads Gains\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jake Maxwell Watts   \par \uc2 316 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Dezember 2013\par 10:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Singapore's benchmark stock index made light gains Tuesday, the last trading day of the year, ending 2013 where it started, while Malaysia retreated slightly from an all-time high.\par \par \uc2 At the close of its half-day session, the FTSE Straits Times Index was 0.5% higher, while Malaysia's KLCI lost 0.3% and Vietnam's Ho Chi Minh index ended up 0.9%. Financial markets in Thailand, Indonesia and the Philippines were closed for national holidays.\par \par \uc2 The year has been a turbulent one for Southeast Asian markets, which were buffeted in August and September by speculation over a possible reduction in bond buying by the U.S. Federal Reserve. More recently, star performers Thailand and the Philippines were dragged lower by street protests and a typhoon, respectively.\par \par \uc2 Thailand ended the year down the most, losing 6.7% to 1298.7, followed by Indonesia's JSX, which closed 1% lower. The theme of the year for Jakarta has been a persistent current account deficit, seen by investors as making Indonesia more vulnerable when global interest rates rise further, as they are expected to do.\par \par \uc2 The year's strongest gainers were Vietnam, up 20.9%, and Malaysia, up 10.5%. Governments in the two countries have pursued very different but similarly welcomed reform agendas that have bolstered investor confidence and pushed markets higher.\par \par \uc2 Among regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Malaysia's ringgit strengthened 0.5% against the U.S. dollar, while \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar gained 0.2% after a record \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 . Changes in the yuan typically influence the Singapore dollar, since Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  manages the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  using an index that includes the yuan.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jake Maxwell Watts at jake.watts@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 31, 2013 04:40 ET (09:40 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131231e9cv000om\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
