\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Fin Min: 'Wise' Central Banks Will Curb  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Volatility\b0\par\par\uc2 By Martin Vaughan   \par \uc2 207 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Dezember 2012\par 06:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2 --Asian central banks should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to temper rapid changes in exchange rates, which could occur with advanced countries keeping monetary stimulus in place, Singapore's Finance Minister Tharman Shanmugaratnam said Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "There is much sense in curtailing volatility in the exchange rate over the short-term," said Mr. Tharman, who also serves as chairman of a key policy-setting committee at the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 His remarks came during an address at the World Islamic Economic Forum in Johor Bahru, Malaysia.\par \par \uc2 If left unchecked, volatile capital flows can lead to asset price bubbles, and sudden outflows can destabilize economies, Mr. Tharman said in his speech.\par \par \uc2 Like central banks in South Korea and the Philippines, which have also seen rapid appreciation in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent months, Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is suspected of buying dollars to slow the Singapore dollar's rise.\par \par \uc2 "There is merit in allowing exchange rates in Asia's emerging economies to appreciate gradually over the long term, reflecting their more rapid growth," Tharman said.\par \par \uc2 "But some stability in the short term is wise," he added.\par \par \uc2 Write to Martin Vaughan at martin.vaughan@dowjones.com [ 04-12-12 0632GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020121204e8c40005o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
