\par\par \b \uc2 INSTANT VIEW 3-Singapore Aug core inflation eases, gives MAS policy room\b0\par\par\uc2 1,036 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2012\par 07:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Sept 24 (Reuters) - Singapore's consumer price index rose by a stronger-than-expected 3.9 percent in August from a year earlier as housing costs continued to increase, though core inflation eased, giving the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  more room to ease policy next month if needed.\par \par \uc2 Economists polled by Reuters had expected August inflation to ease to a two-year low of 3.6 percent from 4.0 percent in July.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore\uc2 's (MAS) core inflation measure rose 2.2 percent year-on-year, slightly less than expected, and 0.2 percent month-on-month, compared with July when core inflation rose 2.4 percent year-on-year and 0.2 percent month-on-month.\par \par \uc2 Core inflation excludes the cost of accommodation and private road transport, which are strongly influenced by government policy, and is the figure the MAS pays more attention to when deciding monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 August's CPI data will be last inflation report before MAS issues its half-year monetary policy statement in mid-October.\par \par \uc2 KEY POINTS:\par \par \uc2 - The Ministry of Trade and Industry and the MAS said in joint statement that core inflation was expected to remain close to the current level over the next few months.\par \par \uc2 - They reiterated their forecast that inflation would average 4.0-4.5 percent in 2012, with core inflation to average 2.5-3 percent. [ID:nS7E8EQ002\}\par \par \uc2 - Housing was the biggest contributor to August inflation, rising 6.1 percent from a year earlier. Accommodation cost inflation, a subset of housing, slowed to 7.4 percent year-on-year in August from 7.8 percent in July as residential property rentals rose at a more moderate pace.\par \par \uc2 COMMENTARY:\par \par \uc2 TIM CONDON\uc2 , HEAD OF RESEARCH FOR ASIA AT ING FINANCIAL MARKETS IN SINGAPORE\par \par \uc2 "For the man on the street, the things that hit their pocket books are things like food, a hike in transport fares or fuel prices, things that economists like to strip out because of their volatility and the fact that they are not really controllable by monetary policy. But those are precisely the ones that hit people most directly. So there's a sense in Singapore that inflation is on the high side."\par \par \uc2 "Our sense is that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore\uc2  is going to retain its modest and gradual policy in October. There is a hit to the economy from the kind of demand shock that hits exports across the entire world. It hits Singapore and there's a risk of technical recession happening, but the practical nature of it is something that the authorities will look through and remain focused on trying to bring down the headline inflation number."\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL WAN, ECONOMIST, \uc2 CREDIT SUISSE\par \par \uc2 "Core inflation went down. I think this number bodes well for the MAS to ease its pace of appreciation, which we have been calling for for some time. We are calling for headline inflation to fall to 3.5 percent by year end.\par \par \uc2 "The scheme of things is not significantly far from current levels but we think that it will still give the MAS some leeway, but the figure will still elevated for some time. I think the impact of QE3 will be marginal. So the focus now has gone to growth much more than inflation."\par \par \uc2 ROBERT PRIOR-WANDESFORDE, HEAD OF INDIA AND SOUTH EAST ASIA ECONOMICS AT \uc2 CREDIT SUISSE\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 "The most important element is the further drop in core inflation so it's getting down to more acceptable levels as far as MAS is concerned. Put that together with growing prospects of a technical recession in Singapore, I think you've got a recipe for policy easing at the meeting in mid-October. That's not withstanding the possible effects of QE3.\par \par \uc2 "At the moment we're going for a slight flattening of the (Singapore dollar) slope, still upwards but a 1-2 percent appreciation pace as opposed to 3-4 percent appreciation which seems to be in place right now.\par \par \uc2 "We think the Singapore dollar at these sort of levels is quite vulnerable. This fall in inflation should give MAS some room to ease."\par \par \uc2 LESLIE TANG, ECONOMIST, OSK-DMG\par \par \uc2 On inflation:\par \par \uc2 "They (MAS) probably can move on monetary policy. Headline inflation is due to domestic policy like accommodation and car prices. MAS can't do anything about it using monetary policy as this requires government policies."\par \par \uc2 On October monetary policy:\par \par \uc2 "The Singapore dollar exchange rate is nearly at the top end of the trading band so MAS might widen the band again to give itself more flexibility instead of having to constantly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 MARKET REACTION:\par \par \uc2 - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar firmed slightly to 1.2269 to the U.S. dollar after the data. Before the inflation figure, the city-state's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at 1.2272, compared with Friday's close of 1.2252. It has risen more than 3 percent so far this year.\par \par \uc2 - The benchmark FTSE Straits Times Index was down almost 0.2 percent after the data, little changed from earlier levels. It has gained roughly 16 percent so far this year amid a strong rally in Southeast Asian markets.\par \par \uc2 BACKGROUND:\par \par \uc2 - August's inflation was the lowest since November 2010 when year-on-year inflation was 3.8 percent.\par \par \uc2 - Singapore manages monetary policy by letting the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise or fall against an undisclosed basket of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and its next half yearly policy statement will be issued in mid-October.\par \par \uc2 At its last policy statement in April, MAS said it will let the Singapore dollar rise at a slightly faster pace as a cushion against inflation.\par \par \uc2 - Singapore's gross domestic product contracted 0.7 percent quarter-on-quarter in the April-June period on a annualised and seasonally adjusted rate as both manufacturing and services slowed.\par \par \uc2 - Singapore's non-oil domestic exports in August fell more than expected, raising the prospect of the city-state slipping into a recession as exports to the European Union plunged.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Singapore and Manila bureaus; Editing by Kim Coghill)\par \par \uc2 (Kevin.Lim@thomsonreuters.com; +65 6403 5663)\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE-ECONOMY/INFLATION (VIEW) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | SI  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120924e89o0005y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
